// ---------- Week-long mini-studies: one passage, a short explanation and one activity each day for 7 days ----------
// Day n unlocks n−1 days after the study is posted in the group; missed days can be caught up at any time.
// Passages are loaded from the Douay-Rheims; explanations are study notes written for Golden Chain.
GC_STUDIES.length=0;
GC_STUDIES.push(
{id:"w-beatitudes",t:"The Beatitudes",e:"⛰️",d:"Seven days with Christ's portrait of the happy life.",days:[
 {t:"The poor in spirit",r:["matthew",5,1,3],ex:"Jesus goes up the mountain like a new Moses and begins the New Law with happiness, not rules. The 'poor in spirit' are those who do not cling to riches or honours and know they depend on God. Thomas places this first because detachment opens the heart to every other gift (ST I-II q.69 a.3).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What do you find hardest to let go of: possessions, reputation, or control? Name one."}},
 {t:"The meek and those who mourn",r:["matthew",5,4,5],ex:"Meekness is not weakness but strength under control: anger ruled by reason and love. Those who mourn are not only the grieving, but those who weep over sin and the world's suffering. Both receive what the proud and the comfortable cannot: the land, and God's own consolation.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"In the Douay-Rheims, which Beatitude comes second?",o:["Blessed are they that mourn","Blessed are the meek","Blessed are the merciful"],a:1,why:"The Douay-Rheims (following the Vulgate) has the meek at verse 4 and those who mourn at verse 5; many modern Bibles swap them."}},
 {t:"Hunger for justice",r:["matthew",5,6,6],ex:"Hunger and thirst are daily, physical, urgent. Jesus asks us to want holiness and right relationships that much: justice towards God (religion) and towards neighbour. The promise is fullness, but only to those who are hungry, not to those who are already full of themselves.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you most hunger for justice right now?",o:["My own holiness","My family","My campus or work","South Africa","The wider world"]}},
 {t:"The merciful",r:["matthew",5,7,7],ex:"Mercy is love meeting misery. It is the one Beatitude whose reward is the same as the act: the merciful obtain mercy. The Church lists fourteen works of mercy, seven corporal (feed, clothe, visit…) and seven spiritual (counsel, forgive, bear wrongs patiently, pray…).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which work of mercy could you do today, for whom?"}},
 {t:"The clean of heart",r:["matthew",5,8,8],ex:"The 'heart' in Scripture is the whole inner person: thoughts, desires and choices. A clean heart is undivided, wanting God above all, and it sees God: dimly now in faith and creation, fully in heaven. Purity of heart is more than chastity, though chastity is part of it.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What is promised to the clean of heart?",o:["They shall possess the land","They shall see God","They shall be comforted"],a:1,why:"\"Blessed are the clean of heart: for they shall see God\" (Matt 5:8): the beatific vision, the final end of man."}},
 {t:"The peacemakers",r:["matthew",5,9,9],ex:"Peace, for Augustine, is the 'tranquillity of order': things in their right place, above all our hearts in God. Peacemakers do not avoid conflict at any price; they work to restore right order, and so they resemble the Son, and are called children of God.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there a relationship in your life that needs peace? What is one small step?"}},
 {t:"The persecuted",r:["matthew",5,10,12],ex:"The last Beatitude returns to the first promise, the kingdom of heaven, closing the circle. Suffering for Christ is not a sign that something has gone wrong but that we belong to the line of the prophets. 'Be glad and rejoice': joy is possible even under insult, because the reward is secure.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which Beatitude will you try to live this coming week?",o:["Poor in spirit","Meek","Mourning","Hunger for justice","Merciful","Clean of heart","Peacemaker","Persecuted"]}}]},
{id:"w-prodigal",t:"Lost and found",e:"🐑",d:"A week in Luke 15: the mercy of the Father.",days:[
 {t:"The lost sheep",r:["luke",15,1,7],ex:"The Pharisees grumble that Jesus eats with sinners, and he answers with three stories of lost things. A shepherd leaves ninety-nine to find one: God's love is personal, not statistical. Notice the joy: heaven rejoices more over one sinner who repents.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"When have you felt 'found' by God?"}},
 {t:"The lost coin",r:["luke",15,8,10],ex:"A coin cannot repent; it can only be searched for. Grace comes first: God lights the lamp and sweeps the house before we even know we are lost. The Fathers saw the woman as the Church, searching with the lamp of the word.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How many groats did the woman have?",o:["Seven","Ten","A hundred"],a:1,why:"Luke 15:8: ten groats; she lost one."}},
 {t:"The son leaves",r:["luke",15,11,16],ex:"Asking for the inheritance early is almost like wishing the father dead. Sin is often this: wanting the gifts without the Giver. Freedom used against love becomes famine; the son ends up envying pigs.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What gift of God have you tried to enjoy without God?"}},
 {t:"He returned to himself",r:["luke",15,17,19],ex:"'Returning to himself' comes before returning to the father. Conversion begins with honesty: seeing my situation as it really is. His motive is still mixed (he is hungry), but it is enough to start walking. Imperfect contrition can lead to confession, where grace completes it.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What usually brings you back to God?",o:["Feeling empty","A friend","Scripture or Mass","Guilt","Beauty or nature","A crisis"]}},
 {t:"The father runs",r:["luke",15,20,24],ex:"The father sees him 'a great way off', so he must have been watching. He runs, embraces, interrupts the rehearsed speech, and restores him with robe, ring and shoes: dignity, authority, freedom. This is the sacrament of Penance: not a court but a homecoming.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would you like to hear the Father say to you?"}},
 {t:"The elder son",r:["luke",15,25,32],ex:"The elder son never left home, yet he too is lost: in resentment. He calls his brother 'this thy son', refusing to call him brother. The parable has no ending: we are not told whether he goes in. It is left to us.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What is the elder son's main problem?",o:["He was lazy","He resents the mercy shown to his brother","He also left home"],a:1,why:"He refuses to share the father's joy (Luke 15:28–30). Envy is sorrow at another's good (II-II q.36)."}},
 {t:"Have mercy on me, O God",r:["psalms",50,3,14],ex:"The Miserere is David's prayer after his great sin, prayed by the Church every Friday in the Office. It asks not only for pardon but for a new heart: 'Create a clean heart in me, O God.' End the week by praying it slowly, and consider going to Confession.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you go to Confession this week?",o:["Yes, already booked","Yes, I'll find a time","Not this week","I'd like help preparing"]}}]},
{id:"w-shepherd",t:"The Lord is my shepherd",e:"🌿",d:"A verse a day of Psalm 22 (23), ending with the Good Shepherd.",days:[
 {t:"I shall want nothing",r:["psalms",22,1,1],ex:"The Douay-Rheims says 'The Lord ruleth me': to be shepherded is to be governed by someone who loves you. 'I shall want nothing' does not mean every wish is granted, but that nothing truly necessary will be lacking.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What are you anxious about lacking right now? Hand it over in one sentence."}},
 {t:"Pasture and water",r:["psalms",22,2,2],ex:"Pasture and refreshing water: rest and nourishment. The Fathers read these as the Scriptures and Baptism. God feeds before he leads; prayer and the sacraments are not extras but food.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where is your 'pasture' this week?",o:["Mass","Scripture","Rosary","Adoration","Time in nature","Good friends"]}},
 {t:"He converted my soul",r:["psalms",22,3,3],ex:"'He hath converted my soul': God turns us back when we stray, and leads us on the paths of justice 'for his own name's sake', not because we earned it. Our holiness is first his glory.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which 'path of justice' is God leading you on at the moment?"}},
 {t:"The shadow of death",r:["psalms",22,4,4],ex:"The psalm changes from 'he' to 'thou' exactly here: in the dark valley, we speak to God face to face. The rod and staff protect and guide. Fear is not denied; it is accompanied.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name a 'valley' you or someone you love is walking through. Pray for it here."}},
 {t:"A table prepared",r:["psalms",22,5,5],ex:"In the presence of enemies, God sets a feast and anoints the head: a picture of honour and joy. Christians have always seen here the Eucharist, the 'chalice which inebriateth', and Confirmation's anointing.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"In most modern Bibles, this psalm is numbered…",o:["22","23","24"],a:1,why:"The Douay-Rheims follows the Vulgate (Greek) numbering, usually one lower than Hebrew-based Bibles from Psalm 10 to 147."}},
 {t:"Mercy will follow me",r:["psalms",22,6,6],ex:"Mercy does not just wait for us; it 'follows' or pursues us all our days. The psalm ends with the goal: to dwell in the house of the Lord, which is the Church now and heaven forever.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where have you seen God's mercy 'following' you this week?"}},
 {t:"I am the good shepherd",r:["john",10,11,16],ex:"Jesus claims the psalm for himself: he is the Lord who shepherds. The difference from a hireling is love unto death: 'the good shepherd giveth his life for his sheep'. He knows his own by name and desires 'one fold and one shepherd'.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which image from this week will you remember?",o:["Pasture","Still water","The valley","The table","Mercy following","The Good Shepherd"]}}]},
{id:"w-bread",t:"The Bread of Life",e:"🍞",d:"A week through John 6 and the Eucharist.",days:[
 {t:"Five loaves and two fishes",r:["john",6,1,15],ex:"A boy's small lunch becomes food for five thousand. God does not need our much, only our all. The leftovers fill twelve baskets: the Twelve will distribute this abundance in the Church.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What small 'loaves' could you give Jesus this week?"}},
 {t:"It is I, fear not",r:["john",6,16,21],ex:"Between the two halves of the chapter, Jesus walks on the sea: the One who commands creation is about to give himself as food. 'It is I' recalls God's name, 'I am'.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Jesus say to the frightened disciples?",o:["Why did you doubt?","It is I. Be not afraid","Follow me"],a:1,why:"John 6:20."}},
 {t:"Work for the food that endures",r:["john",6,26,35],ex:"The crowd wants more free bread. Jesus redirects them: 'Labour not for the meat which perisheth.' The work God asks is faith in the One he sent. Then the great claim: 'I am the bread of life.'",act:{k:"poll",q:"What do you 'labour' for most this year?",o:["Marks or career","Money","Relationships","Fitness","Holiness","Not sure"]}},
 {t:"The living bread from heaven",r:["john",6,41,51],ex:"The manna in the desert fed the body for a day; this bread gives eternal life. And Jesus makes it concrete: 'the bread that I will give is my flesh, for the life of the world.' The Cross and the Eucharist are one gift.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What helps you to believe that Christ is truly present in the Eucharist?"}},
 {t:"Eat my flesh",r:["john",6,52,59],ex:"When the crowd objects, Jesus does not soften the words; he strengthens them, using a word for 'eat' that means chewing. The Church has always read this literally: the Eucharist is truly his Body and Blood (Council of Trent, sess. 13; ST III q.75).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How does Jesus respond when the crowd objects?",o:["He explains it was only a symbol","He repeats the teaching even more strongly","He changes the subject"],a:1,why:"John 6:54–57 (DR): he insists that his flesh is meat indeed and his blood drink indeed."}},
 {t:"To whom shall we go?",r:["john",6,60,69],ex:"Many disciples leave, and Jesus lets them go rather than retract. Peter's answer is the creed of every tired believer: not 'I understand', but 'Lord, to whom shall we go? Thou hast the words of eternal life.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"When has faith felt hard for you, and what kept you?"}},
 {t:"Do this in memory of me",r:["1-corinthians",11,23,29],ex:"Paul hands on what he received: the Last Supper, the Body given and the Blood of the new covenant. So we must 'prove' ourselves, examining our conscience, before receiving. End the week by preparing well for your next Mass.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How will you prepare for your next Mass?",o:["Arrive early and pray","Read the readings beforehand","Go to Confession","Fast an hour properly","Make a thanksgiving afterwards"]}}]},
{id:"w-mary",t:"A week with Mary",e:"🌹",d:"Seven scenes with the Mother of God.",days:[
 {t:"The Annunciation",r:["luke",1,26,38],ex:"Gabriel greets Mary as 'full of grace': already filled by God. Her question is not doubt but seeking understanding; her answer, 'Be it done to me according to thy word', is the most fruitful yes in history.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What might God be asking you to say yes to?"}},
 {t:"The Visitation",r:["luke",1,39,56],ex:"Mary goes 'with haste' to serve. Elizabeth, filled with the Holy Spirit, calls her 'the mother of my Lord', and John leaps in the womb. Mary's Magnificat praises God who lifts up the lowly.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Elizabeth call Mary?",o:["Queen of heaven","The mother of my Lord","My sister"],a:1,why:"Luke 1:43: the root of the title Mother of God (Theotokos, Council of Ephesus, 431)."}},
 {t:"She pondered in her heart",r:["luke",2,1,19],ex:"God enters the world in poverty, and the first to hear are shepherds. Mary 'kept all these words, pondering them in her heart': a model of meditation, turning events over before God.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What event of this week do you need to 'ponder' with God?"}},
 {t:"Finding in the Temple",r:["luke",2,41,52],ex:"Even Mary and Joseph lose sight of Jesus and search 'sorrowing'. Faith includes not understanding: 'they understood not the word'. Still, she keeps these things in her heart.",act:{k:"poll",q:"When you 'lose sight' of Jesus, what helps you find him?",o:["Mass","Confession","Scripture","Prayer with others","Waiting patiently"]}},
 {t:"Do whatever he tells you",r:["john",2,1,11],ex:"At Cana, Mary notices a need before anyone asks and brings it to her Son. Her last words in the Gospels point away from herself: 'Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye.' That is true devotion to Mary.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is one thing Jesus is telling you that you haven't done yet?"}},
 {t:"Behold thy mother",r:["john",19,25,27],ex:"At the Cross, Jesus gives his mother to the beloved disciple, and the disciple to her. The Church has always heard this as given to every disciple: Mary is our mother in the order of grace.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you pray with Mary?",o:["Rosary","Hail Mary","Angelus","Memorare","Not yet, but I'd like to"]}},
 {t:"With Mary in prayer",r:["acts",1,12,14],ex:"After the Ascension, the apostles wait for the Spirit 'with Mary the mother of Jesus', persevering in prayer. She is at the heart of the first Christian community, as she is at the heart of our groups.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write a short prayer to Mary for this group."}}]},
{id:"w-vine",t:"Abide in me",e:"🍇",d:"A week in John 15–17: the vine, friendship and unity.",days:[
 {t:"The true vine",r:["john",15,1,4],ex:"The Father is the vinedresser who prunes fruitful branches. Pruning hurts but makes room for more fruit. 'Abide in me': the Christian life is first remaining connected, not frantic effort.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'pruning' might God be doing in your life?"}},
 {t:"Without me, nothing",r:["john",15,5,8],ex:"'Without me you can do nothing': Thomas teaches that no act deserving eternal life is possible without grace (ST I-II q.109). This frees us from self-reliance and from despair at once.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"'Without me you can do…'",o:["little","nothing","much"],a:1,why:"John 15:5."}},
 {t:"That my joy may be in you",r:["john",15,9,11],ex:"Jesus wants his own joy in us, and our joy filled. Joy is the fruit of charity: rejoicing in God and in our neighbour's good (II-II q.28). Obedience is the way to abide in his love.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What gives you the deepest joy?",o:["Prayer","Friends","Family","Serving others","Beauty","Learning"]}},
 {t:"I have called you friends",r:["john",15,12,17],ex:"Charity is friendship with God (II-II q.23). Jesus no longer calls us servants but friends, because he shares his Father's secrets with us. The command follows: love one another as I have loved you, unto laying down life.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who in this group could you encourage this week, and how?"}},
 {t:"If the world hate you",r:["john",15,18,21],ex:"Friendship with Christ brings opposition. Not every criticism is persecution, but faithfulness will cost something. Jesus says it first so we are not shaken.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where is it hardest to be openly Christian?",o:["Lectures or work","Social media","Family","Friends","Nowhere, thankfully"]}},
 {t:"The Spirit of truth",r:["john",16,7,13],ex:"It is better for us that Jesus goes, because then the Paraclete comes: Advocate, Comforter, the Spirit of truth who 'will teach you all truth'. We are not orphans.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray 'Come, Holy Spirit' and write one thing you ask him for."}},
 {t:"That they all may be one",r:["john",17,20,23],ex:"Jesus prays for us, 'them also who through their word shall believe in me', that we may be one as he and the Father are one, so that the world may believe. Unity is a witness.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would make this group more truly one in Christ?"}}]},
{id:"w-truth",t:"Speak the truth",e:"🗣️",d:"A week on honesty, the tongue and the truth that frees.",days:[
 {t:"Members of one another",r:["ephesians",4,25,25],ex:"Paul's reason for truthfulness is communion: 'we are members one of another'. A lie wounds the body we belong to. Thomas says society would be impossible unless people could believe one another (II-II q.109 a.3).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where are you most tempted to shade the truth?"}},
 {t:"No false witness",r:["exodus",20,16,16],ex:"The eighth commandment protects the neighbour's good name and the trust on which justice depends. It covers lies, perjury, rash judgment, detraction and calumny (CCC 2464–2487).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Revealing someone's real faults without need is called…",o:["calumny","detraction","flattery"],a:1,why:"Detraction reveals real faults without valid reason; calumny lies about them (CCC 2477)."}},
 {t:"The truth shall make you free",r:["john",8,31,32],ex:"Freedom does not come from doing what we like but from living in the truth. Lies, even small ones, entangle us; truth, even when costly, releases.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there a truth you need to tell someone? What is stopping you?"}},
 {t:"Let your yea be yea",r:["matthew",5,33,37],ex:"Jesus wants speech so reliable that oaths become unnecessary. A simple yes or no that can be trusted is a form of holiness.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which do you find hardest?",o:["Avoiding exaggeration","Keeping promises","Not making excuses","Not gossiping","Admitting mistakes"]}},
 {t:"Taming the tongue",r:["james",3,2,10],ex:"James compares the tongue to a bit, a rudder and a spark: small but steering everything. 'By it we bless God… and by it we curse men.' Guarding speech is a daily discipline.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Choose one tongue rule for tomorrow (e.g. 'speak of the absent as if present')."}},
 {t:"A hard case: the midwives",r:["exodus",1,15,21],ex:"The midwives disobey Pharaoh to save the babies and give an evasive answer. Thomas says they were rewarded 'not for their lie, but for their fear of God' (II-II q.110 a.3 ad 2). Courage and truthfulness can both be kept: refuse, conceal, protect, without asserting falsehood.",act:{k:"poll",q:"The murderer at the door asks where your friend is. What would you do?",o:["Refuse and conceal, without lying","Lie to protect","Not sure"]}},
 {t:"What is truth?",r:["john",18,37,38],ex:"Pilate asks 'What is truth?' while Truth stands before him. Jesus came 'to give testimony to the truth'; everyone who is of the truth hears his voice. Truthfulness is ultimately loyalty to a Person.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"One honest sentence about where you are with God right now."}}]},
{id:"w-virtues",t:"The cardinal virtues",e:"⚖️",d:"A week on prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance.",days:[
 {t:"The four hinges",r:["wisdom",8,7,7],ex:"Scripture names the four 'cardinal' (hinge) virtues: temperance, prudence, justice and fortitude. Every good moral act turns on them. They are acquired by practice and also infused by grace.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which do you most need this month?",o:["Prudence","Justice","Fortitude","Temperance"]}},
 {t:"Prudence: count the cost",r:["luke",14,28,30],ex:"Prudence is right reason applied to action: deliberate, judge, then act. Its chief act is command, actually doing what you judged right (II-II q.47 a.8). Planning is not worry; it is virtue.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"The chief act of prudence is…",o:["counsel","judgment","command"],a:2,why:"II-II q.47 a.8: applying to action what was counselled and judged."}},
 {t:"Justice: give each their due",r:["micah",6,8,8],ex:"Justice renders to each their due with a constant will: honest work, fair pay, true words, keeping promises. Micah joins it to mercy and humble walking with God: justice is never cold.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there anything you owe someone (money, thanks, an apology) that you could repay this week?"}},
 {t:"Fortitude: take courage",r:["joshua",1,9,9],ex:"Fortitude's chief act is endurance, to stand firm in danger rather than to attack (II-II q.123 a.6). God's command 'take courage' comes with a reason: 'the Lord thy God is with thee'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What good thing have you been avoiding out of fear?"}},
 {t:"Temperance: run to win",r:["1-corinthians",9,24,27],ex:"Athletes refrain from many things to win a perishable crown. Temperance orders pleasure by the real needs of life (II-II q.141 a.6), not to kill joy but to keep us free.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which small fast will you try this week?",o:["Phone-free evenings","No sugary drinks","No snacking between meals","No series for a day","Friday abstinence"]}},
 {t:"Meek and humble of heart",r:["matthew",11,28,30],ex:"Jesus offers rest, and describes his own heart: meek and humble. Humility, a part of temperance, is the truth about ourselves before God; meekness rules anger. His yoke is sweet because he carries it with us.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need Christ's 'rest' this week?"}},
 {t:"Above all, charity",r:["colossians",3,12,17],ex:"Paul lists the virtues as clothes to 'put on', and over them all charity, 'the bond of perfection'. For Thomas, charity is the form of all the virtues, directing them to God (II-II q.23 a.8).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name one concrete act of love you will do tomorrow."}}]}
);

// ---------- Life's questions as 7-day studies ----------
// Days with q: use the Life's-questions answer (QAS) as the day's thought and link to the full answer.
GC_STUDIES.push(
{id:"w-dating",t:"Dating and courtship",e:"💍",d:"A week on dating, attraction, purity and discerning marriage, from Life's questions.",qcat:"dating",days:[
 {t:"What is dating for?",q:"dating-purpose",r:["tobit",8,4,9],act:{k:"reflect",q:"In one sentence: what do you want dating (or a future relationship) to be for?"}},
 {t:"Attraction and beauty",q:"attraction",r:["genesis",2,18,24],act:{k:"poll",q:"What do you notice first in someone?",o:["Looks","Kindness","Faith","Humour","Intelligence","How they treat others"]}},
 {t:"Can men and women be just friends?",q:"men-women-friends",r:["sirach",6,14,17],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What makes a friendship between a man and a woman healthy?"}},
 {t:"How far is too far?",q:"how-far",r:["1-thessalonians",4,3,7],act:{k:"quiz",q:"For Paul, God's will for us is…",o:["our comfort","our sanctification","our success"],a:1,why:"\"This is the will of God, your sanctification\" (1 Thess 4:3). Purity is about becoming holy, not only avoiding rules."}},
 {t:"How do I know if someone is the one?",q:"the-one",r:["ruth",1,16,17],act:{k:"poll",q:"Which matters most when discerning marriage?",o:["Shared faith","Character and virtue","Friendship","Family support","Attraction","Practical life goals"]}},
 {t:"Dating someone who isn't Catholic",q:"mixed-marriage",r:["1-corinthians",7,12,16],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would you need to talk about honestly before marrying someone of a different faith?"}},
 {t:"Marriage, priesthood or religious life?",q:"vocation",r:["1-corinthians",7,32,35],act:{k:"poll",q:"Have you ever seriously asked God about your vocation?",o:["Yes, and I'm fairly sure","Yes, still discerning","Not yet, but I will","I'd like help with this"]}}]},
{id:"w-love",t:"Love, friendship and marriage",e:"❤️",d:"A week on what love is, true friendship, and the meaning of marriage, from Life's questions.",qcat:"love",days:[
 {t:"What is love, really?",q:"what-is-love",r:["1-corinthians",13,4,8],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Replace 'charity' in verse 4 with your own name. Which phrase is hardest to make true?"}},
 {t:"Choosing good friends",q:"friendship",r:["john",15,13,15],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name one friend who makes you better. Thank them today."}},
 {t:"Whom should I love more?",q:"order-of-love",r:["1-john",4,19,21],act:{k:"quiz",q:"Why do we love God, according to St John?",o:["Because we are good","Because God first hath loved us","Because we fear hell"],a:1,why:"1 John 4:19. Our love is always a response."}},
 {t:"What is marriage?",q:"what-is-marriage",r:["genesis",2,18,24],act:{k:"poll",q:"Which word best describes marriage for you?",o:["Covenant","Friendship","Sacrament","Family","Vocation","Sacrifice"]}},
 {t:"A good husband and father",q:"good-husband",r:["ephesians",5,25,33],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What virtue would you most like to grow in before marriage (or within it)?"}},
 {t:"Why not live together first?",q:"cohabitation",r:["hebrews",13,4,4],act:{k:"poll",q:"How would you explain the Church's view to a friend?",o:["Marriage is a gift that's given completely","It protects both people","It's about trust and commitment","I'm not sure yet"]}},
 {t:"Contraception and NFP",q:"contraception-nfp",r:["genesis",1,27,28],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What questions do you still have about this teaching? (The group can help.)"}}]},
{id:"w-passions",t:"Passions, temptation and confession",e:"🔥",d:"A week on emotions, anger, temperance, lust, envy and the mercy of Confession.",qcat:"appetites",days:[
 {t:"Are emotions bad?",q:"passions-bad",r:["psalms",41,2,6],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which emotion has been strongest for you this week, and what was it telling you?"}},
 {t:"Handling anger",q:"anger",r:["ephesians",4,26,32],act:{k:"poll",q:"What helps you most when you're angry?",o:["Waiting before I speak","Prayer","Exercise","Talking it through","Writing it down"]}},
 {t:"Temperance in food and drink",q:"food-drink",r:["1-corinthians",10,31,31],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would 'eating and drinking to the glory of God' look like for you this week?"}},
 {t:"Overcoming lust",q:"lust-porn",r:["matthew",5,27,30],act:{k:"poll",q:"Which help is most realistic for you?",o:["Phone out of the bedroom","An accountability friend","Regular Confession","Filters","Talking to a counsellor","Daily prayer"]}},
 {t:"Comparing myself to others",q:"envy",r:["genesis",4,3,8],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Whose success do you find hard to celebrate? Pray for them by name."}},
 {t:"Mortal and venial sin",q:"mortal-venial",r:["1-john",5,16,17],act:{k:"quiz",q:"For a sin to be mortal it needs grave matter, full knowledge and…",o:["public scandal","deliberate consent","a priest's judgment"],a:1,why:"CCC 1857: grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent."}},
 {t:"Why confess to a priest?",q:"confession-priest",r:["john",20,21,23],act:{k:"poll",q:"When will you next go to Confession?",o:["This week","This month","I'm nervous: help me prepare","I'll look up times at my parish"]}}]},
{id:"w-faith",t:"Doubt, suffering and prayer",e:"🙏",d:"A week on doubt, evil, prayer, dryness, Mary and the Church.",qcat:"spiritual",days:[
 {t:"I have doubts",q:"doubt",r:["mark",9,22,24],act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is one question about the faith you'd like the group to think about with you?"}},
 {t:"Why does God permit suffering?",q:"evil",r:["romans",8,28,32],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where have you seen good come out of something painful?"}},
 {t:"How do I pray well?",q:"how-pray",r:["luke",11,1,10],act:{k:"poll",q:"When do you pray most easily?",o:["Morning","At Mass","Walking or commuting","Night","With others","Hardly ever (yet)"]}},
 {t:"When prayer feels empty",q:"dryness",r:["psalms",21,2,6],act:{k:"reflect",q:"Jesus prayed this psalm on the Cross. What does that tell you about dry or dark times?"}},
 {t:"Do Catholics worship Mary?",q:"worship-mary",r:["luke",1,46,49],act:{k:"quiz",q:"In the Magnificat, Mary says all generations shall…",o:["worship her","call her blessed","obey her"],a:1,why:"Luke 1:48. Honour (veneration) is not worship (adoration), which is for God alone."}},
 {t:"Scripture and Tradition",q:"sola-scriptura-short",r:["2-thessalonians",2,14,14],act:{k:"reflect",q:"How would you answer a friend who asks why Catholics need more than the Bible?"}},
 {t:"Who do you say that I am?",r:["matthew",16,15,19],ex:"Every question this week comes back to this one. Peter answers by revelation, not flesh and blood, and Jesus builds his Church on him and gives him the keys. Our doubts, sufferings and prayers are carried inside that Church, which the gates of hell will not overcome.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Answer Jesus's question in your own words: who is he for you?"}}]}
);

// ---------- Seasonal 7-day studies (the Church's year and the student year) ----------
// Each study has when(d): true when it is in season, so the picker can suggest it first.
function gcDOY(d){return new Date(d.getFullYear(),d.getMonth(),d.getDate());}
function gcBetween(d,a,b){const t=gcDOY(d);return t>=gcDOY(a)&&t<=gcDOY(b);}
function gcMD(d,m1,d1,m2,d2){const y=d.getFullYear();const a=new Date(y,m1-1,d1),b=new Date(y,m2-1,d2);return a<=b?gcBetween(d,a,b):(gcBetween(d,a,new Date(y,11,31))||gcBetween(d,new Date(y,0,1),b));}
function gcEaster(d,from,to){const E=easterDate(d.getFullYear());return gcBetween(d,addDays(E,from),addDays(E,to));}
function gcCK(d,from,to){const ck=addDays(adventStart(d.getFullYear()),-7);return gcBetween(d,addDays(ck,from),addDays(ck,to));}
const GCR=(b,c,f,t)=>[b,c,f,t];
GC_STUDIES.push(
{id:"s-souls",t:"Saints and holy souls",e:"🕯️",d:"November: the communion of saints, purgatory and praying for the dead.",when:d=>gcMD(d,10,25,11,15),season:"All Saints and All Souls",days:[
 {t:"A great multitude",r:GCR("revelation",7,9,14),ex:"John sees a crowd no one can number, from every nation, in white robes washed in the blood of the Lamb. These are the saints: not only the famous ones, but every soul now with God. All Saints' Day (1 November) celebrates them all.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which saint, canonised or not (a grandparent, a teacher), has shown you what holiness looks like?"}},
 {t:"A cloud of witnesses",r:GCR("hebrews",12,1,2),ex:"The saints surround us like spectators in a stadium, cheering us on. The communion of saints means the Church in heaven, the Church being purified, and the Church on earth are one family who pray for one another (CCC 954–959).",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which saint do you most often ask to pray for you?",o:["Mary","St Joseph","St Thomas Aquinas","St Augustine","My patron saint","I'd like to choose one"]}},
 {t:"A holy thought to pray for the dead",r:GCR("2-maccabees",12,43,46),ex:"Judas Maccabeus has sacrifices offered for fallen soldiers 'that they might be loosed from sins'. This is the oldest scriptural witness to praying for the dead, which the Church has done from the beginning (CCC 1032).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write the names of people who have died for whom you will pray this week."}},
 {t:"Saved, yet so as by fire",r:GCR("1-corinthians",3,11,15),ex:"Paul describes works tested by fire: some burn, yet the builder 'shall be saved, yet so as by fire'. The Church sees here purgatory: not a second chance, but the final purification of those who die in God's friendship (CCC 1030–1031).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Purgatory is for…",o:["people who rejected God","those who die in God's grace but still need purification","everyone forever"],a:1,why:"CCC 1030: all who die in God's grace and friendship but still imperfectly purified are assured of salvation, and undergo purification."}},
 {t:"In the hand of God",r:GCR("wisdom",3,1,9),ex:"'The souls of the just are in the hand of God.' Death looks like defeat, 'but they are in peace'. This reading is used at funerals because it gives Christian grief its shape: real sorrow, but not without hope.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How does this passage change the way you think about someone you have lost?"}},
 {t:"I am the resurrection",r:GCR("john",11,21,27),ex:"At Lazarus's tomb, Martha's grief and faith meet Jesus: 'I am the resurrection and the life.' He asks her, and us, 'Believest thou this?' Martha answers with one of the great creeds of the Gospels.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you pray for the dead?",o:["At Mass","Eternal rest prayer","Rosary","Visiting the grave","I haven't, but I will"]}},
 {t:"That you be not sorrowful",r:GCR("1-thessalonians",4,12,17),ex:"Christians grieve, but Paul writes 'that you be not sorrowful, even as others who have no hope'. Paul ends: 'comfort ye one another with these words.' From 1 to 8 November, a plenary indulgence for the holy souls can be gained by visiting a cemetery and praying for the dead (see today's Mass page).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who could you comfort this week? Send them a message."}}]},
{id:"s-king",t:"Christ the King",e:"👑",d:"The last week of the Church's year: the King who reigns from the Cross.",when:d=>gcCK(d,-9,3),season:"Christ the King",days:[
 {t:"One like the son of man",r:GCR("daniel",7,13,14),ex:"Daniel sees one 'like the son of man' receive a kingdom that shall never be destroyed. Jesus takes this title for himself more than any other. The feast of Christ the King closes the liturgical year by looking to his final coming.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would change if Christ were truly King of your week?"}},
 {t:"My kingdom is not of this world",r:GCR("john",18,33,37),ex:"Before Pilate, Jesus is a king who came 'to give testimony to the truth'. His kingdom does not come from this world's power, but it is meant to transform this world from within.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where does Christ most need to reign in your life?",o:["My time","My phone","My relationships","My studies or work","My thoughts","My money"]}},
 {t:"The firstborn of every creature",r:GCR("colossians",1,12,20),ex:"Paul's hymn: in Christ all things were created and in him they hold together; he is head of the Church and reconciles all things by the blood of his Cross. His kingship is cosmic.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Through what does Christ reconcile all things?",o:["His teaching","The blood of his Cross","His miracles"],a:1,why:"Colossians 1:20: 'making peace through the blood of his cross'."}},
 {t:"The King who judges by love",r:GCR("matthew",25,31,46),ex:"At the end, the King judges by works of mercy, and identifies himself with the hungry, the stranger, the sick and the prisoner. 'As long as you did it to one of these my least brethren, you did it to me.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which of 'the least' is in front of you this week?"}},
 {t:"Remember me in thy kingdom",r:GCR("luke",23,35,43),ex:"The sign above the Cross mocks him as King, but the thief sees truly: 'Lord, remember me when thou shalt come into thy kingdom.' The King's throne is the Cross; his first subject is a repentant criminal.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the thief's prayer slowly, with your own name."}},
 {t:"He comes with the clouds",r:GCR("revelation",1,5,8),ex:"Christ is 'the faithful witness, the first begotten of the dead, and the prince of the kings of the earth'. He loved us and washed us from our sins. 'I am Alpha and Omega', the beginning and the end.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Next Sunday is the First Sunday of Advent. Will you start a group Advent study?",o:["Yes!","Maybe","I'll do one on my own"]}},
 {t:"Every knee shall bow",r:GCR("philippians",2,5,11),ex:"Christ's way to kingship is self-emptying: obedient unto death. 'Therefore God also hath exalted him.' We share his kingship by serving.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"One act of hidden service you will do this week."}}]},
{id:"s-advent",t:"Advent: prepare the way",e:"🕯️",d:"The first weeks of Advent: waiting, watching and preparing.",when:d=>{const y=d.getFullYear();const a=adventStart(y);return gcBetween(d,addDays(a,-3),new Date(y,11,16));},season:"Advent",days:[
 {t:"Comfort ye my people",r:GCR("isaiah",40,1,11),ex:"Advent begins with comfort and a voice crying in the wilderness: 'Prepare ye the way of the Lord.' God is coming, and he comes as a shepherd who carries the lambs.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'valley' in your life needs to be filled, or 'mountain' made low, before Christmas?"}},
 {t:"A rod out of the root of Jesse",r:GCR("isaiah",11,1,10),ex:"From a stump comes a shoot: the Messiah, on whom rests the Spirit of wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, godliness and fear of the Lord. These are the seven gifts we receive in Confirmation.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How many gifts of the Spirit are listed in Isaiah 11 (Douay-Rheims)?",o:["Five","Seven","Twelve"],a:1,why:"Isaiah 11:2–3 in the Vulgate tradition lists seven: wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, godliness (piety) and fear of the Lord."}},
 {t:"John the Baptist",r:GCR("mark",1,1,8),ex:"John prepares the way by preaching repentance. Advent is a quieter Lent: a season for Confession and for clearing room in the heart.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How will you prepare this Advent?",o:["Confession","Daily Scripture","Less phone","Advent wreath at home","A work of mercy","Rorate or early Mass"]}},
 {t:"A virgin shall conceive",r:GCR("isaiah",7,10,14),ex:"Ahaz refuses to ask for a sign, so God gives one anyway: a virgin will bear a son called Emmanuel, God with us. Matthew sees it fulfilled in Mary.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need 'God with us' most right now?"}},
 {t:"It is now the hour",r:GCR("romans",13,11,14),ex:"'It is now the hour for us to rise from sleep… The night is passed, and the day is at hand.' St Augustine read this passage at his conversion. Advent is the season to wake up.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What are you 'asleep' to that you want to wake up to?"}},
 {t:"Watch ye therefore",r:GCR("matthew",24,37,44),ex:"Christ comes in three ways, said St Bernard: in the flesh at Bethlehem, in grace now, and in glory at the end. Advent prepares for all three.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which coming are you most preparing for?",o:["Christmas","Christ in my daily life","His return at the end","All three"]}},
 {t:"Swords into ploughshares",r:GCR("isaiah",2,1,5),ex:"All nations stream to the mountain of the Lord to learn his ways, and weapons become farm tools. Advent hope is not only private: it is for peace in families, cities and nations, South Africa included.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for peace in one place you care about."}}]},
{id:"s-christmas-eve",t:"The last week before Christmas",e:"⭐",d:"16 to 24 December: Joseph, Zachary, Mary, Elizabeth and the coming Child.",when:d=>gcMD(d,12,15,12,24),season:"Christmas novena",days:[
 {t:"Joseph's dream",r:GCR("matthew",1,18,25),ex:"Joseph, 'being a just man', plans to put Mary away quietly, until the angel speaks. He obeys at once and takes Mary home. Silent, faithful obedience is his gift to Christmas.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is God asking you to do quietly, without fuss?"}},
 {t:"Zachary in the Temple",r:GCR("luke",1,5,17),ex:"An old priest is told his prayer is heard: John will be born to go before the Lord 'in the spirit and power of Elias'. Zachary doubts and is struck silent until the birth.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What do you find hardest to believe God will do?",o:["Answer my prayers","Change me","Heal a relationship","Use me","Forgive me"]}},
 {t:"Be it done to me",r:GCR("luke",1,26,38),ex:"Mary's yes makes Christmas possible. The Church prays the Angelus three times a day to remember this moment.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the Angelus once today. What line stays with you?"}},
 {t:"The babe leaped",r:GCR("luke",1,39,45),ex:"Two pregnant women meet, and the unborn John leaps for joy before the unborn Christ. Elizabeth's words, 'Blessed art thou among women', became part of the Hail Mary.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Which words of the Hail Mary come from Elizabeth?",o:["Hail, full of grace","Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb","Holy Mary, Mother of God"],a:1,why:"Luke 1:42; 'Hail, full of grace' is Gabriel's greeting (1:28)."}},
 {t:"The Magnificat",r:GCR("luke",1,46,56),ex:"Mary's song turns the world upside down: the proud scattered, the mighty put down, the humble exalted, the hungry filled. The Church sings it every evening at Vespers.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write one line of thanks to God in the spirit of the Magnificat."}},
 {t:"The Benedictus",r:GCR("luke",1,67,79),ex:"Zachary's tongue is loosed and he blesses God: 'the Orient from on high hath visited us, to enlighten them that sit in darkness.' The Church sings it every morning at Lauds.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you pray Morning Prayer one day this week?",o:["Yes","Already do","I'll try Night Prayer instead"]}},
 {t:"A child is born to us",r:GCR("isaiah",9,2,7),ex:"'For a child is born to us… and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, God the Mighty, the Father of the world to come, the Prince of Peace.' Tomorrow is Christmas.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which name of the Child speaks most to you this Christmas?"}}]},
{id:"s-christmas",t:"The Christmas octave",e:"🌟",d:"25 December to early January: the Word made flesh.",when:d=>gcMD(d,12,25,1,5),season:"Christmas",days:[
 {t:"Born in Bethlehem",r:GCR("luke",2,1,14),ex:"God enters history under Caesar Augustus, in a borrowed manger, and the angels sing 'Glory to God in the highest; and on earth peace to men of good will.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What surprised you about this Christmas?"}},
 {t:"The Word was made flesh",r:GCR("john",1,1,14),ex:"John's Christmas Gospel goes back before time: the Word who was with God and was God 'was made flesh, and dwelt among us'. At these words Catholics traditionally kneel in the Creed on Christmas Day.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"On which feasts do we kneel at 'and was incarnate' in the Creed?",o:["Every Sunday","Christmas and the Annunciation","Easter"],a:1,why:"The rubrics direct kneeling on Christmas and the Annunciation; on other Sundays we bow."}},
 {t:"The shepherds' haste",r:GCR("luke",2,15,20),ex:"The shepherds go 'with haste', find the Child, and return 'glorifying and praising God'. Mary keeps all these words in her heart.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Who will you tell about Christmas joy this week?",o:["Family","Friends","Someone lonely","This group","Online"]}},
 {t:"The Holy Innocents",r:GCR("matthew",2,13,18),ex:"Christmas has a shadow: Herod's massacre of the children of Bethlehem (28 December). The Church honours them as martyrs, and prays for all children threatened today.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for children who suffer violence or are unwanted. Name a place or cause."}},
 {t:"Simeon's song",r:GCR("luke",2,22,35),ex:"Old Simeon holds the Child and can die in peace: 'Now thou dost dismiss thy servant.' He also warns Mary of a sword. We pray his song every night at Compline.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would let you say tonight, 'Now dismiss thy servant in peace'?"}},
 {t:"God hath spoken by his Son",r:GCR("hebrews",1,1,6),ex:"God spoke 'at sundry times and in divers manners' by the prophets, but last of all by his Son, the brightness of his glory. The angels adore him.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What has God been saying to you this Christmas?"}},
 {t:"Born of a woman",r:GCR("galatians",4,4,7),ex:"'God sent his Son, made of a woman', so that we might receive adoption as sons. On 1 January the Church celebrates Mary, Mother of God, and prays for peace.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What will you ask of God for the new year?",o:["Holiness","Peace","Health","Good studies or work","A good relationship","Faith for my family"]}}]},
{id:"s-new-year",t:"New beginnings",e:"🌅",d:"January: starting the year with God.",when:d=>gcMD(d,1,1,1,31),season:"New Year",days:[
 {t:"His mercies are new every morning",r:GCR("lamentations",3,22,26),ex:"Written amid ruins, these verses still trust that God's mercies have not failed: 'They are new every morning, great is thy faithfulness.' Every day, not only every year, is a fresh start.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What do you want to leave behind from last year?"}},
 {t:"A new creature",r:GCR("2-corinthians",5,17,17),ex:"'If then any be in Christ a new creature, the old things are passed away.' Resolutions help, but real newness is grace: Baptism, Confession and the Eucharist.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which spiritual resolution are you making this year?",o:["Daily prayer","Daily Scripture (keep the streak!)","Monthly Confession","Weekday Mass","Rosary","An Academy course"]}},
 {t:"Behold I do new things",r:GCR("isaiah",43,18,19),ex:"'Remember not former things… Behold I do new things.' God is not only behind us in memory; he is ahead of us, opening a way in the desert.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What new thing might God be doing in your life this year?"}},
 {t:"A time for everything",r:GCR("ecclesiastes",3,1,8),ex:"'All things have their season.' Wisdom is knowing which season you are in: study, rest, mourning, celebration, speaking or silence.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which season are you in?",o:["Building","Waiting","Healing","Celebrating","Searching","Hard work"]}},
 {t:"Forgetting the things behind",r:GCR("philippians",3,12,14),ex:"Paul has not 'arrived', but he presses on toward the prize. Perseverance, says Thomas, is persisting in good despite the length of the road (II-II q.137).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write one goal for this year and one first step."}},
 {t:"Seek first the kingdom",r:GCR("matthew",6,33,33),ex:"Priorities are the real resolution: 'Seek ye therefore first the kingdom of God… and all these things shall be added unto you.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would 'seeking the kingdom first' look like in your weekly timetable?"}},
 {t:"Teach us to number our days",r:GCR("psalms",89,12,17),ex:"Psalm 89 (90) asks God to 'make thy right hand known… men learned in heart, in wisdom' and to 'direct thou the works of our hands'. Offer the whole year to him.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray: 'Lord, direct the work of my hands this year', and add your own words."}}]},
{id:"s-epiphany",t:"Epiphany and the Baptism of the Lord",e:"✨",d:"Early January: Christ revealed to the nations.",when:d=>gcMD(d,1,6,1,20),season:"Epiphany",days:[
 {t:"The Magi",r:GCR("matthew",2,1,12),ex:"Wise men from the East follow a star and worship the Child with gold, frankincense and myrrh: gifts for a king, for God, and for one who will die. Epiphany means 'showing': Christ shown to all nations.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which gift would you bring?",o:["Gold: my best work","Frankincense: my prayer","Myrrh: my suffering offered"]}},
 {t:"Arise, be enlightened",r:GCR("isaiah",60,1,6),ex:"'Arise, be enlightened, O Jerusalem: for thy light is come.' Nations walk in its light and bring gold and frankincense. The Church sees Isaiah fulfilled in the Magi.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need Christ's light this year?"}},
 {t:"Kings shall adore him",r:GCR("psalms",71,10,11),ex:"'The kings of Tharsis and the islands shall offer presents… all kings of the earth shall adore him.' This is why the Magi are often called kings.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Epiphany means…",o:["Gift","Showing or manifestation","Star"],a:1,why:"Greek epiphaneia, a manifestation: Christ revealed to the Gentiles."}},
 {t:"The Baptism of the Lord",r:GCR("matthew",3,13,17),ex:"Jesus, sinless, steps into the Jordan with sinners. The heavens open, the Spirit descends, and the Father speaks: the Trinity revealed. Thomas says Christ's baptism gave water the power to sanctify (ST III q.39).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Do you know the date of your baptism? Find it and celebrate it this year."}},
 {t:"Behold the Lamb of God",r:GCR("john",1,29,34),ex:"John points away from himself: 'Behold the Lamb of God… who taketh away the sin of the world.' The priest says these words at every Mass before Communion.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What sin or burden do you want the Lamb to take away?"}},
 {t:"The first sign at Cana",r:GCR("john",2,1,11),ex:"The third 'epiphany' the Church celebrates is Cana, where 'he manifested his glory, and his disciples believed in him'.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What 'water' would you like Jesus to turn into wine?",o:["My studies or work","A relationship","My prayer life","My family","My health"]}},
 {t:"The mystery revealed",r:GCR("ephesians",3,2,6),ex:"Paul's 'mystery' is that the Gentiles are co-heirs in Christ. The Church is catholic: for every nation, language and people, including ours.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for someone you know who doesn't yet know Christ."}}]},
{id:"s-lent",t:"Lent: return to me",e:"🌵",d:"The first weeks of Lent: prayer, fasting and almsgiving.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,-49,-22),season:"Lent",days:[
 {t:"Rend your hearts",r:GCR("joel",2,12,18),ex:"'Be converted to me with all your heart, in fasting, and in weeping… rend your hearts, and not your garments.' Ash Wednesday's reading: Lent is about the heart, not only the ashes.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What are you giving up or taking on this Lent?",o:["Social media","A food or drink","Extra prayer","Daily Scripture","Almsgiving","A work of mercy"]}},
 {t:"Almsgiving in secret",r:GCR("matthew",6,1,4),ex:"Jesus names three Lenten practices: almsgiving, prayer and fasting, all done 'in secret' for the Father who sees. Almsgiving frees the heart from attachment to money.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who could you help this Lent, without anyone knowing?"}},
 {t:"Prayer in secret",r:GCR("matthew",6,5,6),ex:"'Enter into thy chamber, and having shut the door, pray to thy Father in secret.' A fixed place and time help more than good intentions.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where and when will you pray each day this Lent?"}},
 {t:"Fasting with joy",r:GCR("matthew",6,16,18),ex:"'When thou fastest anoint thy head, and wash thy face.' Fasting is not misery for its own sake; it trains desire and makes room for God. Ash Wednesday and Good Friday are days of fasting and abstinence.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Which two days are days of fasting for Catholics?",o:["Every Friday","Ash Wednesday and Good Friday","Holy Thursday and Easter Sunday"],a:1,why:"Ash Wednesday and Good Friday (CCC 2043; can. 1251). Fridays of Lent are days of abstinence."}},
 {t:"Forty days in the desert",r:GCR("matthew",4,1,11),ex:"Jesus is tempted three ways: bread (pleasure), the Temple jump (pride), and the kingdoms (power). He answers each time with Scripture. Lent's forty days echo his forty days.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which temptation is strongest for you?",o:["Comfort and pleasure","Pride and attention","Control and power"]}},
 {t:"Is not this the fast I have chosen?",r:GCR("isaiah",58,6,9),ex:"God's chosen fast is justice: loosing bonds, feeding the hungry, sheltering the homeless. Then 'thy light shall break forth as the morning'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"One concrete act of justice or mercy you will do this week."}},
 {t:"Nineveh repents",r:GCR("jonah",3,1,10),ex:"The most reluctant prophet gives the shortest sermon, and a whole city repents in sackcloth. God 'had mercy'. No one is too far gone.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you go to Confession during Lent?",o:["Yes, this week","Yes, before Holy Week","I'm nervous","Already been"]}}]},
{id:"s-lent2",t:"Lent: living water, light and life",e:"💧",d:"Later Lent: the great Gospels of conversion.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,-21,-8),season:"Lent",days:[
 {t:"The woman at the well",r:GCR("john",4,5,26),ex:"Jesus asks a Samaritan woman for a drink and offers her 'living water'. He knows her whole past and still seeks her. The catechumens hear this Gospel before Easter baptism.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'thirst' do you try to satisfy with things that don't last?"}},
 {t:"The man born blind",r:GCR("john",9,1,11),ex:"Jesus heals a man blind from birth with clay and the pool of Siloe ('Sent'). The healed man sees more and more clearly; the Pharisees become more and more blind.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need Christ to open your eyes?"}},
 {t:"Lazarus, come forth",r:GCR("john",11,32,44),ex:"'Jesus wept.' Then he calls a dead man out of the tomb. Confession is our Lazarus moment: called out of the grave of sin and unbound.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What 'grave clothes' do you need loosed?",o:["A habit","A grudge","Fear","Shame","Laziness","An addiction"]}},
 {t:"The Pharisee and the publican",r:GCR("luke",18,9,14),ex:"One man prays about himself; the other only says 'O God, be merciful to me a sinner.' The second goes home justified. Humility is the doorway of prayer.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the publican's prayer slowly ten times today."}},
 {t:"Seventy times seven",r:GCR("matthew",18,21,35),ex:"Peter asks how often to forgive; Jesus says without limit, and tells of a servant forgiven a fortune who will not forgive a few coins. Forgiven people forgive.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there someone you need to forgive? Name them before God."}},
 {t:"Come, let us return",r:GCR("hosea",6,1,6),ex:"'I desired mercy, and not sacrifice: and the knowledge of God more than holocausts.' Lent's practices only matter if they make us more merciful.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How has Lent changed you so far?"}},
 {t:"The suffering servant",r:GCR("isaiah",53,3,7),ex:"'Surely he hath borne our infirmities and carried our sorrows… by his bruises we are healed.' Holy Week is about to begin.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which Holy Week liturgy will you attend?",o:["Palm Sunday","Holy Thursday","Good Friday","Easter Vigil","All of them!"]}}]},
{id:"s-holy-week",t:"Holy Week",e:"✝️",d:"Palm Sunday to Holy Saturday, day by day.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,-7,-1),season:"Holy Week",days:[
 {t:"Palm Sunday",r:GCR("matthew",21,1,11),ex:"Jesus enters Jerusalem on a donkey, a king of peace, and the crowd cries 'Hosanna'. Within days some will cry 'Crucify'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where are you tempted to cheer Jesus in public and abandon him in private?"}},
 {t:"Anointing at Bethania",r:GCR("john",12,1,8),ex:"Mary pours precious ointment on Jesus's feet and 'the house was filled with the odour'. Love that seems wasteful to Judas is the right response to God.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'precious ointment' could you pour out for Jesus this week?"}},
 {t:"Holy Thursday: he washed their feet",r:GCR("john",13,1,15),ex:"'Having loved his own… he loved them unto the end.' Jesus washes feet, institutes the Eucharist and the priesthood, and gives the new commandment.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you spend time at the altar of repose on Holy Thursday night?",o:["Yes","I'll try","Not possible this year"]}},
 {t:"Gethsemane",r:GCR("luke",22,39,46),ex:"'Not my will, but thine be done.' Jesus's sweat becomes 'as drops of blood'. He accepts the cup out of love for the Father and for us.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is the 'cup' in your life you find hardest to accept?"}},
 {t:"Good Friday: It is consummated",r:GCR("john",19,16,30),ex:"Jesus carries his Cross, is crucified between thieves, gives his mother to John, and dies saying 'It is consummated.' Today we fast and venerate the Cross.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write a short prayer of thanks to Christ crucified."}},
 {t:"By his bruises we are healed",r:GCR("isaiah",53,3,7),ex:"The servant is 'led as a sheep to the slaughter' and opens not his mouth. Thomas: the Passion saves as merit, satisfaction, sacrifice and redemption (ST III q.48).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Which is NOT one of Thomas's four ways the Passion saves (III q.48)?",o:["Merit","Satisfaction","Sacrifice","Good example only"],a:3,why:"Thomas lists merit, satisfaction, sacrifice and redemption; example is real but not the only way."}},
 {t:"Holy Saturday: the tomb",r:GCR("matthew",27,57,66),ex:"Joseph of Arimathea buries Jesus; the women sit facing the tomb; guards seal it. The Church waits in silence by the tomb, and then the Easter Vigil begins in the dark.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What are you waiting for God to raise to life?"}}]},
{id:"s-easter",t:"The Easter octave",e:"🌅",d:"Easter Sunday to Divine Mercy Sunday: he is risen!",when:d=>gcEaster(d,0,8),season:"Easter",days:[
 {t:"The empty tomb",r:GCR("john",20,1,9),ex:"Mary Magdalene runs to Peter and John; John 'saw, and believed'. The linen cloths are lying there. Christ is risen!",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What does the Resurrection change about your life this week?"}},
 {t:"Emmaus",r:GCR("luke",24,13,35),ex:"Two disciples walk away from Jerusalem in sadness; a stranger opens the Scriptures and their hearts burn; they know him 'in the breaking of bread'. That is the shape of every Mass: Word, then Eucharist.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where has your heart 'burned' this Easter?",o:["At Mass","Reading Scripture","In this group","In nature","In someone's kindness"]}},
 {t:"Peace be to you",r:GCR("john",20,19,23),ex:"The risen Lord breathes on the apostles: 'Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them.' The Easter gift is the sacrament of mercy.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who needs your 'peace be to you' this week?"}},
 {t:"Doubting Thomas",r:GCR("john",20,24,29),ex:"Thomas wants proof; Jesus offers his wounds, and Thomas makes the highest confession in the Gospels: 'My Lord, and my God.' 'Blessed are they that have not seen, and have believed.'",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Thomas say when he sees the risen Jesus?",o:["It is the Lord","My Lord, and my God","Rabboni"],a:1,why:"John 20:28: the clearest confession of Christ's divinity in the Gospels."}},
 {t:"Breakfast by the sea",r:GCR("john",21,1,14),ex:"After a night of failed fishing, a word from the shore fills the nets with 153 fish, and the risen Jesus cooks breakfast. He meets us in ordinary work.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is Jesus 'on the shore' of your ordinary week?"}},
 {t:"If Christ be not risen",r:GCR("1-corinthians",15,12,22),ex:"Paul stakes everything on the Resurrection: 'if Christ be not risen again, then is our preaching vain.' But Christ is risen, 'the firstfruits of them that sleep'.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How would you explain the Resurrection to a sceptical friend?",o:["The empty tomb","The witnesses","The changed apostles","The martyrs","I'd need help"]}},
 {t:"God hath raised him up",r:GCR("acts",2,22,24),ex:"Peter's first sermon: 'God hath raised him up, having loosed the sorrows of hell.' The octave ends on Divine Mercy Sunday, with a plenary indulgence (see the Mass page).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray 'Jesus, I trust in you' and name what you trust him with."}}]},
{id:"s-pentecost",t:"Ascension to Pentecost",e:"🔥",d:"The nine days of prayer for the Holy Spirit.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,38,50),season:"Pentecost",days:[
 {t:"The Ascension",r:GCR("acts",1,6,11),ex:"Jesus ascends, and the angels ask: 'Why stand you looking up to heaven?' The first novena begins: nine days of prayer with Mary for the Spirit.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you pray a Pentecost novena with the group?",o:["Yes","I'll pray 'Come, Holy Spirit' daily","Maybe"]}},
 {t:"Another Paraclete",r:GCR("john",14,15,21),ex:"'I will ask the Father, and he shall give you another Paraclete, that he may abide with you for ever.' Paraclete means advocate, helper, comforter.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you most need a Helper right now?"}},
 {t:"Pentecost",r:GCR("acts",2,1,11),ex:"A mighty wind, tongues of fire, and the apostles speak so that every nation hears in its own language: the undoing of Babel, and the birthday of the Church.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the Veni Creator or 'Come, Holy Spirit' and ask for one gift."}},
 {t:"The fruit of the Spirit",r:GCR("galatians",5,16,25),ex:"The works of the flesh against the fruit of the Spirit: charity, joy, peace, patience, benignity, goodness, longanimity, mildness, faith, modesty, continency, chastity. The Douay-Rheims lists twelve.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which fruit do you most want to grow?",o:["Charity","Joy","Peace","Patience","Goodness","Mildness","Chastity"]}},
 {t:"Abba, Father",r:GCR("romans",8,14,17),ex:"The Spirit makes us sons and daughters who cry 'Abba, Father'. Prayer is not a technique but the Spirit praying in us.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Talk to God as 'Abba' for five minutes. What did you say?"}},
 {t:"Diversities of graces",r:GCR("1-corinthians",12,4,11),ex:"One Spirit gives different gifts 'to every one… for profit'. Every member of this group has gifts meant for the others.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name a gift you see in someone in this group, and tell them."}},
 {t:"The seven gifts",r:GCR("isaiah",11,2,3),ex:"Wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety and fear of the Lord. Thomas teaches that the gifts make us docile to the Spirit's promptings, beyond what virtue alone can do (I-II q.68).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"For Thomas, the gifts of the Spirit make us…",o:["independent of God","docile (ready) to be moved by the Spirit","perfect without effort"],a:1,why:"I-II q.68 a.1: gifts are dispositions making us readily movable by the Holy Spirit."}}]},
{id:"s-sacred-heart",t:"Corpus Christi and the Sacred Heart",e:"❤️‍🔥",d:"June: the Body of Christ and the Heart that loved us.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,55,72)||gcMD(d,6,1,6,30),season:"June: Corpus Christi and the Sacred Heart",days:[
 {t:"The blood of the covenant",r:GCR("exodus",24,3,8),ex:"Moses seals the covenant with blood: 'This is the blood of the covenant.' Jesus will take these words and make them his own at the Last Supper.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What does 'covenant' mean to you: a contract, or something more?"}},
 {t:"This is my body",r:GCR("luke",22,14,20),ex:"'This is my body, which is given for you. Do this for a commemoration of me.' Corpus Christi celebrates this gift with processions in the streets.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How could you honour the Eucharist this week?",o:["Adoration","Weekday Mass","Genuflect properly","Make a good thanksgiving","Join a procession"]}},
 {t:"One bread, one body",r:GCR("1-corinthians",10,16,17),ex:"'We, being many, are one bread, one body, all that partake of one bread.' The Eucharist makes the Church; Communion with Christ is communion with one another.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How should receiving Communion change how you treat others in this group?"}},
 {t:"Blood and water",r:GCR("john",19,31,37),ex:"A soldier opens Christ's side, and blood and water flow. The Fathers saw the sacraments, Baptism and Eucharist, flowing from his Heart, and the Church born from his side as Eve from Adam.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray before an image of the Sacred Heart, or a crucifix. What do you say?"}},
 {t:"Learn of me",r:GCR("matthew",11,25,30),ex:"Jesus describes his Heart: 'meek, and humble of heart'. The Sacred Heart devotion is simply love answering love.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Do you have an image of the Sacred Heart at home?",o:["Yes","No, but I'd like one","I'd like to enthrone it with my family"]}},
 {t:"A new heart",r:GCR("ezekiel",36,25,28),ex:"'I will give you a new heart… I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh.' The Heart of Jesus is the promise kept.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where has your heart become 'stony'? Ask for a new one."}},
 {t:"Love answering love",r:GCR("1-john",4,19,21),ex:"'Let us therefore love God: because God first hath loved us.' The Act of Reparation on the feast of the Sacred Heart carries a plenary indulgence when prayed publicly.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"One way you will 'love back' this week."}}]},
{id:"s-peter-paul",t:"Sts Peter and Paul",e:"🗝️",d:"Late June: the two pillars of the Church.",when:d=>gcMD(d,6,20,7,5),season:"Sts Peter and Paul (29 June)",days:[
 {t:"Launch out into the deep",r:GCR("luke",5,1,11),ex:"After a failed night, Peter obeys and the nets break; he falls down: 'Depart from me, for I am a sinful man.' Jesus answers: 'From henceforth thou shalt catch men.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is Jesus asking you to 'launch out into the deep'?"}},
 {t:"Thou art Peter",r:GCR("matthew",16,15,19),ex:"Jesus builds his Church on Peter and gives him the keys. The Pope, Peter's successor, is the visible principle of the Church's unity (CCC 882).",act:{k:"poll",q:"How often do you pray for the Pope?",o:["Daily","At Mass","Sometimes","Rarely: I'll start"]}},
 {t:"Lovest thou me?",r:GCR("john",21,15,19),ex:"Three denials, three questions: 'Simon son of John, lovest thou me?' Each answer is a commission: 'Feed my sheep.' Failure is not the end.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Answer Jesus's question honestly: do you love him? What is in the way?"}},
 {t:"Saul on the road",r:GCR("acts",9,1,19),ex:"The persecutor is thrown down by light: 'Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?' Christ so identifies with his Church that to persecute it is to persecute him.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Who baptised Saul?",o:["Peter","Ananias","Barnabas"],a:1,why:"Acts 9:17–18."}},
 {t:"Peter freed from prison",r:GCR("acts",12,1,11),ex:"'Prayer was made without ceasing by the church unto God for him', and an angel opens the prison. The prayer of a community is powerful.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who in this group, or beyond, needs the group's prayer this week?"}},
 {t:"Peter heals the lame man",r:GCR("acts",3,1,10),ex:"'Silver and gold I have none; but what I have, I give thee: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, arise, and walk.' The Church's riches are not money but Christ.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What can you give someone this week that money cannot buy?"}},
 {t:"I have finished my course",r:GCR("2-timothy",4,6,8),ex:"Paul, near martyrdom: 'I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith.' Both apostles died in Rome under Nero.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'good fight' are you fighting right now?"}}]},
{id:"s-assumption",t:"The Assumption",e:"👑",d:"August: Mary taken up body and soul into heaven.",when:d=>gcMD(d,8,1,8,31),season:"August: the Assumption (15 August)",days:[
 {t:"A woman clothed with the sun",r:GCR("revelation",12,1,6),ex:"'A great sign appeared in heaven: A woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars.' The Church reads this of Mary and of the Church.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What does it mean to you that a human being is already body and soul in heaven?"}},
 {t:"Christ the firstfruits",r:GCR("1-corinthians",15,20,26),ex:"Christ rose first; 'then they that are of Christ'. The Assumption (defined 1950) is Mary sharing early in her Son's resurrection, a pledge of ours.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"In which year was the Assumption defined as dogma?",o:["1854","1950","1965"],a:1,why:"Pius XII, Munificentissimus Deus, 1 November 1950. 1854 was the Immaculate Conception."}},
 {t:"The queen stood at thy right hand",r:GCR("psalms",44,10,16),ex:"The royal wedding psalm: the queen in gilded clothing. The Church sees Mary as Queen Mother at the right hand of the King.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you honour Mary as Queen?",o:["Salve Regina","Rosary","Regina Caeli in Easter","Crowning a statue","Not yet"]}},
 {t:"Arise, my love",r:GCR("song-of-songs",2,10,13),ex:"'Arise, make haste, my love, my dove, my beautiful one, and come.' The liturgy uses the Song of Songs for Mary: the soul totally united to God.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is God saying to you, 'Arise and come'?"}},
 {t:"The Magnificat again",r:GCR("luke",1,46,49),ex:"'All generations shall call me blessed.' On her feast, the Church does exactly what Mary prophesied.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the Magnificat slowly. Which line is yours today?"}},
 {t:"Behold thy mother",r:GCR("john",19,25,27),ex:"The Mother given at the Cross is the same Mother now in glory, still caring for her children.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Entrust one person to Mary's care today."}},
 {t:"A new heaven and a new earth",r:GCR("revelation",21,1,5),ex:"'Behold, I make all things new.' Mary in heaven shows what God intends for all creation: no more death, mourning or pain.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What do you look forward to most in heaven?",o:["Seeing God","Meeting the saints","Loved ones","No more suffering","I'm not sure"]}}]},
{id:"s-joseph",t:"St Joseph",e:"🔨",d:"March: the guardian of the Redeemer (19 March).",when:d=>gcMD(d,3,10,3,25),season:"St Joseph (19 March)",days:[
 {t:"A just man",r:GCR("matthew",1,18,25),ex:"Joseph is called 'a just man'. He never speaks a word in the Gospels; he simply obeys, at once, every time.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where could you practise Joseph's quiet obedience?"}},
 {t:"Flight into Egypt",r:GCR("matthew",2,13,15),ex:"Joseph rises in the night and takes the Child and his mother into exile. He is patron of migrants and refugees, and of all who protect the vulnerable.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for refugees and migrants in South Africa."}},
 {t:"He shall be called a Nazarene",r:GCR("matthew",2,19,23),ex:"Joseph settles the family in obscure Nazareth. Holiness grows in ordinary work, family life and hidden years.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Joseph was a craftsman. Offer your work to God this week?",o:["Yes, every morning","I'll try","I need help with this"]}},
 {t:"Seeking him sorrowing",r:GCR("luke",2,41,52),ex:"Mary says 'thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing'. Joseph shares the anxiety of every parent, and the obedience of Jesus 'subject to them'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for your father, or a father figure."}},
 {t:"Go to Joseph",r:GCR("genesis",41,37,44),ex:"Pharaoh sets Joseph of Egypt over all the land, and later tells the hungry: 'Go to Joseph.' The Church applies this to St Joseph, steward of God's household.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Who said 'Go to Joseph' (Ite ad Ioseph)?",o:["Jesus","Pharaoh","Moses"],a:1,why:"Genesis 41:55, of Joseph of Egypt; the Church applies it to St Joseph."}},
 {t:"A house for David",r:GCR("2-samuel",7,12,16),ex:"God promises David an everlasting throne. Joseph, 'of the house of David', gives Jesus his legal Davidic lineage.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'house' (family, community) is God asking you to build?"}},
 {t:"Patron of a happy death",r:GCR("psalms",115,15,15),ex:"'Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.' Joseph is believed to have died with Jesus and Mary beside him, and is patron of a good death.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray: 'Jesus, Mary and Joseph, assist me in my last agony.'"}}]},
{id:"s-rosary",t:"The Rosary month",e:"📿",d:"October: walking through the mysteries with Mary.",when:d=>gcMD(d,10,1,10,24),season:"October, month of the Rosary",days:[
 {t:"Joyful: the Annunciation",r:GCR("luke",1,26,38),ex:"Each mystery is a Gospel scene to contemplate while praying ten Hail Marys. The Rosary is the Gospel on beads.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How often do you pray the Rosary?",o:["Daily","Weekly","Sometimes","Rarely","Never yet"]}},
 {t:"Luminous: the Transfiguration",r:GCR("luke",9,28,36),ex:"On the mountain Jesus's face shines; the Father says 'This is my beloved Son; hear him.' St John Paul II added the Luminous mysteries in 2002.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Who added the Luminous mysteries?",o:["St Pius V","St John Paul II","Pope Francis"],a:1,why:"Rosarium Virginis Mariae, 2002."}},
 {t:"Sorrowful: the Agony",r:GCR("luke",22,39,46),ex:"The Sorrowful mysteries walk with Christ from Gethsemane to Calvary. They are prayed on Tuesdays and Fridays.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Offer one decade for someone who is suffering. Who?"}},
 {t:"Glorious: the Resurrection",r:GCR("matthew",28,1,10),ex:"'He is not here, for he is risen.' The Glorious mysteries move from the Resurrection to Mary crowned in heaven, prayed on Wednesdays and Sundays.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which glorious mystery gives you most hope?"}},
 {t:"Glorious: Pentecost",r:GCR("acts",2,1,4),ex:"The third Glorious mystery: the Spirit descends on the apostles gathered with Mary.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Would this group pray a decade together on a call?",o:["Yes!","Maybe","I'd rather pray alone"]}},
 {t:"Glorious: the Coronation",r:GCR("revelation",12,1,1),ex:"The fifth Glorious mystery: Mary crowned Queen of heaven and earth. The crown of twelve stars recalls the twelve tribes and twelve apostles.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray a Hail, Holy Queen for this group."}},
 {t:"Pray the whole Rosary",r:GCR("luke",1,46,49),ex:"End the week by praying a full Rosary (five decades), alone or together. Prayed in a church or with family, it carries a plenary indulgence.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Did you pray a full Rosary this week?",o:["Yes","Yes, with others","Part of it","Not yet"]}}]},
{id:"s-exams",t:"Exam season",e:"📚",d:"For May–June and October–November exams: study, stress and trust.",when:d=>gcMD(d,5,15,6,20)||gcMD(d,10,15,11,20),season:"Exam season",days:[
 {t:"Ask for wisdom",r:GCR("james",1,5,6),ex:"'If any of you want wisdom, let him ask of God, who giveth to all men abundantly.' Begin every study session with a short prayer. St Thomas's prayer before study is on the Prayers page.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which exam are you most worried about?",o:["A theory module","Statistics or methods","A language","An essay","Practicals","I'm fine!"]}},
 {t:"Seek her as silver",r:GCR("proverbs",2,1,6),ex:"'If thou shalt seek her as money, and shalt dig for her as for a treasure: then shalt thou understand.' Studiousness is a virtue: diligent effort ordered to truth (II-II q.166).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is one study habit you will improve this week?"}},
 {t:"Be not solicitous",r:GCR("matthew",6,25,34),ex:"Jesus does not forbid planning; he forbids anxious worry about tomorrow. 'Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof.' Plan the day, then trust God with the rest.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write tomorrow's plan in three lines, and hand the outcome to God."}},
 {t:"The peace of God",r:GCR("philippians",4,4,9),ex:"'Be nothing solicitous: but in every thing, by prayer and supplication, with thanksgiving, let your petitions be made known to God. And the peace of God… keep your hearts.'",act:{k:"poll",q:"What helps you most with exam stress?",o:["Prayer","Exercise","Sleep","Study group","Breaks","Talking to someone"]}},
 {t:"They shall run and not be weary",r:GCR("isaiah",40,28,31),ex:"'They that hope in the Lord shall renew their strength… they shall run and not be weary.' Rest and sleep are not wasted time: the body and mind need them.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How many hours will you sleep tonight? Commit to it."}},
 {t:"My help is from the Lord",r:GCR("psalms",120,1,8),ex:"'My help is from the Lord, who made heaven and earth.' A psalm of pilgrims, perfect for exam mornings.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for each person in this group writing exams, by name."}},
 {t:"Not the spirit of fear",r:GCR("2-timothy",1,7,7),ex:"'God hath not given us the spirit of fear: but of power, and of love, and of sobriety.' Whatever the results, your worth is not your marks.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write one sentence to your future self for the day results come out."}}]}
);
// the existing Marian study is in season in May (and the Rosary study in October)
(function(){const m=GC_STUDIES.find(s=>s.id==="w-mary");if(m){m.when=d=>gcMD(d,5,1,5,31)||gcMD(d,12,6,12,10);m.season="May, month of Mary";}})();
function gcStudiesInSeason(d){d=d||new Date();return GC_STUDIES.filter(s=>s.when&&s.when(d));}
GC_STUDIES.push(
{id:"s-candlemas",t:"Light for the nations",e:"🕯️",d:"Late January to Lent: the Presentation (2 Feb), Lourdes (11 Feb) and being light.",when:d=>{const E=easterDate(d.getFullYear());return gcBetween(d,new Date(d.getFullYear(),0,25),addDays(E,-47));},season:"Candlemas and Lourdes",days:[
 {t:"The Presentation",r:GCR("luke",2,22,32),ex:"Forty days after Christmas, Mary and Joseph present Jesus in the Temple. Simeon calls him 'a light to the revelation of the Gentiles'. Candles are blessed on this feast (Candlemas), the end of the Christmas cycle.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How could you offer your week to God, as Mary and Joseph offered Jesus?"}},
 {t:"The Lord comes to his temple",r:GCR("malachi",3,1,4),ex:"'Presently the Lord, whom you seek… shall come to his temple.' Malachi's prophecy is fulfilled when the Child is carried in. He comes like a refiner's fire to purify.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What needs refining in you?",o:["My speech","My use of time","My thoughts","My relationships","My prayer"]}},
 {t:"I am the light of the world",r:GCR("john",8,12,12),ex:"'He that followeth me walketh not in darkness, but shall have the light of life.' The baptismal candle is lit from the Easter candle for this reason.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is there darkness in your life that needs his light?"}},
 {t:"You are the light of the world",r:GCR("matthew",5,14,16),ex:"Christ is the light; we are lights by sharing his. 'So let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"One good work you will do this week so that someone sees God's goodness."}},
 {t:"A light of the Gentiles",r:GCR("isaiah",42,1,7),ex:"The Servant 'shall not cry… the bruised reed he shall not break'. He is gentle with the weak, and given 'for a light of the Gentiles: that thou mightest open the eyes of the blind'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who is a 'bruised reed' around you who needs gentleness?"}},
 {t:"He healed many",r:GCR("mark",1,29,34),ex:"Jesus lifts Peter's mother-in-law by the hand, and in the evening the whole city comes with its sick. Our Lady of Lourdes (11 February) is the World Day of the Sick.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray for someone who is sick, and contact them if you can."}},
 {t:"Is any man sick among you?",r:GCR("james",5,13,16),ex:"'Let him bring in the priests of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil.' The Anointing of the Sick is for serious illness and old age, not only the moment of death.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"The Anointing of the Sick is…",o:["only for the dying","for anyone seriously ill or elderly","for minor colds"],a:1,why:"CCC 1514: it is not only for those at the point of death; serious illness or old age is the fitting time."}}]},
{id:"s-easter-season",t:"The first Christians",e:"🕊️",d:"The Easter season: a week in the Acts of the Apostles.",when:d=>gcEaster(d,9,37),season:"Easter season",days:[
 {t:"One heart and one soul",r:GCR("acts",4,32,35),ex:"The first Christians shared everything so that 'neither was there any one needy among them'. The Resurrection made a new kind of community.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What could this group share more generously (time, help, notes, meals)?"}},
 {t:"The first deacons",r:GCR("acts",6,1,7),ex:"When the Greek widows are neglected, the apostles choose seven men 'full of the Holy Ghost' to serve. Every complaint is an opportunity for charity and good order.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you serve in your parish or community?",o:["Altar server or reader","Choir","Youth ministry","Charity work","Not yet: I'd like to"]}},
 {t:"Philip and the Ethiopian",r:GCR("acts",8,26,39),ex:"An African official reads Isaiah and asks, 'And how can I, unless some man shew me?' Philip explains and baptises him on the road. Africa receives the Gospel in the Church's first generation.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who helped you understand the Scriptures? Thank them."}},
 {t:"God is not a respecter of persons",r:GCR("acts",10,34,48),ex:"Peter realises that 'in every nation, he that feareth him… is acceptable to him', and the Spirit falls on the Gentile household of Cornelius.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Whom do you find it hard to welcome? Pray for them."}},
 {t:"Singing at midnight",r:GCR("acts",16,25,34),ex:"Beaten and chained, Paul and Silas sing hymns at midnight. An earthquake opens the doors, and the jailer and his household are baptised.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would it mean to praise God in your 'midnight' right now?"}},
 {t:"Paul in Athens",r:GCR("acts",17,22,31),ex:"Paul begins with what the Athenians already seek: an altar 'to the unknown God'. Thomas's way is similar: start from reason and creation, and lead to Christ.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What do your friends already 'seek' that could lead them to God?"}},
 {t:"I am the way",r:GCR("john",14,1,6),ex:"'Let not your heart be troubled… I am the way, and the truth, and the life.' The Easter season ends at Pentecost; the way continues in us.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which first-Christian habit will you take up?",o:["Daily prayer","Sharing with the needy","Breaking bread together","Telling others","Learning the faith"]}}]},
{id:"s-holidays",t:"Holiday rest",e:"🏖️",d:"July holidays: rest, Sabbath and time with God.",when:d=>gcMD(d,7,1,7,31)||gcMD(d,12,1,12,5),season:"Holidays",days:[
 {t:"Come apart and rest",r:GCR("mark",6,30,32),ex:"After a busy mission, Jesus says: 'Come apart into a desert place, and rest a little.' Rest is not laziness; it is obedience.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would real rest look like for you this week?"}},
 {t:"Keep holy the Sabbath",r:GCR("exodus",20,8,11),ex:"God himself rested on the seventh day. The Lord's Day is for worship, family, mercy and rest (CCC 2184–2188).",act:{k:"poll",q:"How will you keep Sunday this week?",o:["Mass and a family meal","Time outdoors","Visiting someone","No study or work","Reading something good"]}},
 {t:"Pasture and still waters",r:GCR("psalms",22,2,3),ex:"'He hath brought me up, on the water of refreshment: he hath converted my soul.' Let the Good Shepherd lead you to rest.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is your 'water of refreshment'?"}},
 {t:"Elias under the juniper tree",r:GCR("1-kings",19,4,8),ex:"The prophet is exhausted and wants to die. God's answer is not a lecture but food, water and sleep, twice. Then 'he walked in the strength of that food forty days'.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Are you more tired than you admit? What does your body need?"}},
 {t:"He went up alone to pray",r:GCR("matthew",14,22,23),ex:"Jesus sends the crowds away and goes up the mountain alone to pray. Holidays are a chance to pray longer and more freely.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Will you try a longer time of prayer this week?",o:["30 minutes of adoration","A walk with the Rosary","A whole Gospel in one sitting","A visit to a shrine"]}},
 {t:"Martha and Mary",r:GCR("luke",10,38,42),ex:"Martha is 'busy about much serving'; Mary sits at the Lord's feet. 'Mary hath chosen the best part.' Even good busyness can crowd out the one thing necessary.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What 'much serving' could you drop this week to sit with the Lord?"}},
 {t:"He giveth to his beloved sleep",r:GCR("psalms",126,1,2),ex:"'Unless the Lord build the house, they labour in vain that build it… when he shall give sleep to his beloved.' Trust lets us rest.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Hand one worry about next term to God."}}]},
{id:"s-creation",t:"Season of creation",e:"🌍",d:"September to St Francis (4 October): God the Creator and our care for creation.",when:d=>gcMD(d,9,1,10,4),season:"Season of Creation",days:[
 {t:"In the beginning",r:GCR("genesis",1,1,5),ex:"'In the beginning God created heaven, and earth.' Creation is not a myth of struggle but a free gift of a good God. Thomas: creation is the very dependence of all being on God (ST I q.45).",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Go outside for five minutes. What do you notice that you usually miss?"}},
 {t:"Very good",r:GCR("genesis",1,26,31),ex:"Man and woman are made in God's image and given dominion, which means stewardship, not exploitation. 'God saw all the things that he had made, and they were very good.'",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which act of care for creation is realistic for you?",o:["Less waste","Saving water and power","Walking more","Planting something","Picking up litter"]}},
 {t:"In wisdom hast thou made them all",r:GCR("psalms",103,24,30),ex:"'How great are thy works, O Lord? thou hast made all things in wisdom.' Every creature depends on God's breath: 'thou shalt send forth thy spirit, and they shall be created.'",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which part of creation most moves you to praise God?"}},
 {t:"Where wast thou?",r:GCR("job",38,1,11),ex:"God answers Job's suffering not with an explanation but with the vastness of creation: 'Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth?' Humility before mystery.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What do you need to trust God with that you cannot understand?"}},
 {t:"The birds of the air",r:GCR("matthew",6,26,30),ex:"'Behold the birds of the air… consider the lilies of the field.' Creation teaches trust: the God who clothes the grass will care for us.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What worries you most this month?",o:["Studies","Money","Family","Health","The future","Relationships"]}},
 {t:"Creation groaneth",r:GCR("romans",8,19,23),ex:"All creation 'groaneth and travaileth in pain', waiting to be set free with us. Our salvation is not escape from the world but the world's renewal.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you see creation 'groaning' in South Africa?"}},
 {t:"Praise him, sun and moon",r:GCR("psalms",148,1,13),ex:"Every creature praises God simply by being what it is. St Francis's Canticle of the Creatures (feast 4 October) echoes this psalm: Brother Sun, Sister Moon.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write your own line of praise: 'Praise him, ___'."}}]}
);

// ---------- 7-day studies for every Christian ----------
// Scripture-first studies written so that Catholic, evangelical and non-denominational friends can do them together.
// Faithful to Catholic teaching, but in plain Bible language: no insider vocabulary is needed to take part.
// Passages are the Douay-Rheims; where its spelling of a name differs (Elias, Isai, Sidrach…), the explanation gives the familiar form.
GC_STUDIES.push(
{id:"e-anxiety",all:1,t:"Do not be anxious",e:"🌿",d:"A week with what Jesus and the apostles say to a worried heart.",days:[
 {t:"Look at the birds",r:["matthew",6,25,34],ex:"\"Be not solicitous\" is old English for \"do not be anxious\". Jesus does not tell us our needs are unimportant; he says the Father already knows them (v. 32). Worry tries to carry tomorrow today. Jesus gives us one day's load at a time and points us to something bigger to seek first.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Jesus tell us to seek first?",o:["Security for tomorrow","The kingdom of God, and his justice","The approval of others"],a:1,why:"Matthew 6:33. When the kingdom comes first, everything else finds its proper size."}},
 {t:"Pray instead of worry",r:["philippians",4,4,9],ex:"Paul wrote this from prison. His answer to anxiety is not \"try harder to feel calm\" but \"in every thing, by prayer… with thanksgiving\" bring it to God. The promise is not that every problem disappears, but that God's peace will guard (\"keep\") your heart and mind. Verse 8 adds something practical: choose what you dwell on.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Turn one worry into a prayer: write it out, and add one thing you're thankful for."}},
 {t:"Cast your care",r:["1-peter",5,6,10],ex:"To \"cast\" is to throw something off yourself and onto someone else, the way you'd throw a heavy bag onto a cart. Peter's reason is simple: \"he hath care of you.\" Humility here means admitting you can't carry it alone. Notice that Peter also says to stay alert (v. 8): handing worries to God is not the same as giving up.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What do you worry about most often?",o:["Studies or work","Money","Family","Relationships","My future","My health"]}},
 {t:"Peace, be still",r:["mark",4,35,40],ex:"The disciples were experienced fishermen and still thought they would drown. Jesus was asleep in the same boat, in the same storm. Their question, \"doth it not concern thee?\", is the one anxiety always asks. Jesus answers by calming the sea, and then asks a better question: \"Why are you fearful?\" Having Jesus in the boat doesn't prevent every storm, but it changes how the storm ends.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What storm are you in right now? Ask Jesus the disciples' question honestly, then sit quietly for one minute."}},
 {t:"I will hold your hand",r:["isaiah",41,10,13],ex:"God speaks to a people in exile: \"Fear not, for I am with thee.\" The cure for fear is not a promise that nothing hard will happen; it is a Person who stays. Verse 13 is almost tender: the Lord takes you \"by the hand\". Read it slowly, as if it were addressed to you by name.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"In verse 13, what does the Lord say he does?",o:["Takes thee by the hand","Removes every enemy at once","Gives thee a sign in the sky"],a:0,why:"\"I am the Lord thy God, who take thee by the hand, and say to thee: Fear not.\""}},
 {t:"Not as the world gives",r:["john",14,27,27],ex:"The world's peace usually means \"nothing is wrong\". Jesus said this on the night before he died, when everything was about to go wrong. His peace is something he gives, not a mood we produce. A note for the group: anxiety is not a lack of faith. When worry becomes constant, keeps you from sleeping or stops you living normally, it may be an anxiety disorder, which is common and very treatable. Talking to a doctor or counsellor is wise, not unspiritual (in South Africa, SADAG at sadag.org can help you find support).",act:{k:"poll",q:"What helps you most when you feel anxious?",o:["Praying","Talking to someone","Exercise or a walk","Scripture","Sleep and rest","Writing it down"]}},
 {t:"A place prepared for you",r:["john",14,1,3],ex:"\"Let not your heart be troubled\" comes with a reason: Jesus is preparing a place for you and will come back for you. Our deepest fear is that we will be left alone in the end. Jesus promises the opposite: \"where I am, you also may be.\" Finish the week by trusting him with the very last thing you're afraid of.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What has changed in how you handle worry this week?"}}]},

{id:"e-identity",all:1,t:"Who you are in Christ",e:"🪪",d:"Seven days on the identity God gives you, beyond grades, followers and failures.",days:[
 {t:"Chosen before the world began",r:["ephesians",1,3,8],ex:"Before you achieved anything or failed at anything, God chose you \"in him\" and adopted you as his child. Your worth is not something you earn by performance; it was given to you in Christ. Paul piles up the words (blessed, chosen, adopted, redeemed, forgiven) because no single word is big enough.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which word in this passage do you most need to believe about yourself today?"}},
 {t:"Fearfully and wonderfully made",r:["psalms",138,13,16],ex:"(This is Psalm 139 in most Bibles.) God knew you while you were being formed, the parts you like and the parts you don't. \"Thy eyes did see my imperfect being\": he saw you before you were finished, and he still sees you. You are not an accident or a mistake.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you most often look for your worth?",o:["Achievements","What others think","My appearance","Being needed","Social media","God's love"]}},
 {t:"A new creation",r:["2-corinthians",5,17,21],ex:"\"If then any be in Christ a new creature.\" Your past does not get the final word. Christ took our sin so that we might share his goodness (v. 21). That's the great exchange. It also gives you a job: you're an ambassador, carrying God's message of reconciliation to others.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What old label do you need to leave behind? Write the new one God gives instead."}},
 {t:"Christ lives in me",r:["galatians",2,19,20],ex:"Paul's identity was not \"the best Pharisee\" or \"the former persecutor\". It was \"Christ liveth in me.\" Notice how personal the end of verse 20 is: \"who loved me and delivered himself for me.\" Not just \"the world\". Me.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How does Paul describe the Son of God in verse 20?",o:["Who judged me and forgave me","Who loved me and delivered himself for me","Who chose me because I was faithful"],a:1,why:"Galatians 2:20. The Christian life starts from being loved first."}},
 {t:"Children of God",r:["1-john",3,1,3],ex:"\"Behold what manner of charity the Father hath bestowed upon us\": we are not only called God's children, \"we are now the sons of God.\" John says it is already true now, and that the best is still to come, when we shall see him as he is.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How would you live differently this week if you really believed you are God's beloved child?"}},
 {t:"Abba, Father",r:["romans",8,14,17],ex:"\"Abba\" is the word a child uses for their dad. The Spirit doesn't make us nervous servants hoping not to be fired. He makes us sons and daughters who can call God \"Father\" with confidence. And children are heirs: what belongs to Christ is promised to us with him.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How easy is it for you to think of God as a loving Father?",o:["Very easy","Fairly easy","Hard: my picture of a father is complicated","I'm learning"]}},
 {t:"A chosen people",r:["1-peter",2,9,10],ex:"Identity in Christ is never only private. Peter calls us a people: chosen, royal, holy, \"called… out of darkness into his marvelous light\". You belong to a family that stretches across churches, countries and centuries, and it has a purpose: to \"declare his virtues\".",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who helped you know God's love? Thank them this week."}}]},

{id:"e-grace",all:1,t:"Amazing grace",e:"✝️",d:"A week on God's free gift: mercy we could never earn.",days:[
 {t:"Saved by grace",r:["ephesians",2,4,10],ex:"Grace means a gift God gives freely, one we don't deserve and can't earn. \"By grace you are saved through faith… it is the gift of God. Not of works.\" Catholics and evangelicals both hold to this. Verse 10 then shows where the gift leads: we are God's \"workmanship\", made for the good works he has prepared for us.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"According to verse 8, salvation is…",o:["a reward for good works","the gift of God","earned by religious effort"],a:1,why:"Ephesians 2:8. Good works are the fruit of grace (v. 10), never the price of it."}},
 {t:"While we were still sinners",r:["romans",5,6,11],ex:"We tend to think God will love us once we've cleaned up. Paul says the opposite: Christ died for us \"when as yet we were sinners\" and even \"enemies\". God did not wait for us to become lovable. That's what makes grace amazing.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there something you think makes you too far gone for God? Bring it to him in prayer today."}},
 {t:"Remember me",r:["luke",23,39,43],ex:"The thief on the cross had no time left to do good works, go to church or fix his life. He simply turned to Jesus: \"Lord, remember me.\" And Jesus answered: \"This day thou shalt be with me in paradise.\" It's never too late to turn to Christ.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which picture of grace moves you most?",o:["The thief on the cross","The prodigal son","The woman at the well","Peter forgiven after denying Jesus","Paul the persecutor"]}},
 {t:"Not by our own works",r:["titus",3,4,7],ex:"\"Not by the works of justice which we have done, but according to his mercy, he saved us.\" Paul ties this mercy to being washed and renewed by the Holy Spirit (v. 5); Christians have long read the \"laver of regeneration\" as baptism. Being justified (made right with God) is \"by his grace\" from start to finish.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where are you still trying to earn God's love instead of receiving it?"}},
 {t:"All have sinned",r:["romans",3,21,26],ex:"\"For all have sinned\": the religious and the irreligious, the good student and the rebel. There is \"no distinction\". That's bad news for our pride and good news for our hope, because the same verse continues: we are \"justified freely by his grace\". The ground is level at the foot of the Cross.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How does Paul say we are justified in verse 24?",o:["Freely by his grace","By keeping the whole law","By our family's faith"],a:0,why:"\"Being justified freely by his grace, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.\""}},
 {t:"My grace is sufficient",r:["2-corinthians",12,7,10],ex:"Paul begged three times for his \"thorn\" to go away. God said no, and gave something better: \"My grace is sufficient for thee: for power is made perfect in infirmity.\" Grace isn't only forgiveness for the past; it is strength for today, especially where we are weakest.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you feel weakest right now? Ask for grace there, not only for the problem to vanish."}},
 {t:"Go, and sin no more",r:["john",8,3,11],ex:"The crowd brought her shame; Jesus brought mercy. \"Neither will I condemn thee.\" But grace doesn't leave her where it found her: \"Go, and now sin no more.\" That is the whole shape of grace: received freely, and then lived out. Mercy first, then a new life.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which is harder for you?",o:["Believing I'm forgiven","Forgiving myself","Changing afterwards","Showing grace to others"]}}]},

{id:"e-spirit",all:1,t:"The Holy Spirit",e:"🕊️",d:"Seven days getting to know the Helper Jesus promised.",days:[
 {t:"Another Helper",r:["john",14,15,17],ex:"\"Paraclete\" means helper, advocate or comforter: someone called to your side. Jesus promises \"another\" one, like himself, who will not only be with us but \"in\" us. The Holy Spirit is not a force or a feeling; he is God, a Person, who lives in those who belong to Christ.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How long does Jesus say the Paraclete will abide with us?",o:["Until Pentecost","For ever","Until we sin"],a:1,why:"John 14:16: \"that he may abide with you for ever.\""}},
 {t:"He will remind you",r:["john",14,26,26],ex:"The Spirit is our teacher. He \"will teach you all things and bring all things to your mind\" that Jesus said. Ever had a verse come to mind exactly when you needed it? That is part of his work. He also guided the apostles as they handed on what Jesus taught.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"When has a verse or a word from God come to mind at just the right moment?"}},
 {t:"Wind and fire",r:["acts",2,1,4],ex:"At Pentecost, the frightened disciples became bold witnesses. Wind and fire are pictures of the Spirit: unseen but powerful, warming and purifying. The Church was born that day, and the gift of the Spirit was poured out on \"every one of them\", not on a few leaders only.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which picture of the Holy Spirit speaks to you most?",o:["Wind","Fire","A dove","Living water","Breath","A helper beside me"]}},
 {t:"The fruit of the Spirit",r:["galatians",5,16,25],ex:"Paul contrasts what our selfish nature produces with what the Spirit grows in us. Fruit isn't forced; it grows from staying connected. Your Bible may list nine fruits (love, joy, peace, patience…); the Douay-Rheims follows the Latin and lists twelve. Either way, the point is the same: \"If we live in the Spirit, let us also walk in the Spirit.\"",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which fruit of the Spirit would you most like God to grow in you? Ask for it every day this week."}},
 {t:"When you don't know how to pray",r:["romans",8,26,27],ex:"Sometimes we have no words. Paul says that's exactly where the Spirit helps: he \"asketh for us with unspeakable groanings\". Your silent sigh, your tears, your \"I don't know what to say\" can become prayer. You're never praying alone.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you usually pray when words won't come?",o:["I sit in silence","I use a written prayer","I pray a Psalm","I just say Jesus' name","I usually stop praying"]}},
 {t:"Gifts for the whole body",r:["1-corinthians",12,4,11],ex:"Different gifts, \"but the same Spirit\". The Spirit gives gifts \"unto profit\", that is, to build others up, not to make us look impressive. No Christian has every gift, and no Christian has none. Your gift is needed by the people around you.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Why is the manifestation of the Spirit given, according to verse 7?",o:["To prove who is holiest","Unto profit (for the good of all)","Only to apostles"],a:1,why:"1 Corinthians 12:7. Gifts are for serving the body."}},
 {t:"Power to be witnesses",r:["acts",1,8,8],ex:"Jesus' last promise before ascending: \"you shall receive the power of the Holy Ghost… and you shall be witnesses unto me.\" The Spirit gives power for a purpose: to make Jesus known, starting where you are (\"Jerusalem\") and reaching outward.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where is your \"Jerusalem\": the place where God is asking you to be his witness right now?"}}]},

{id:"e-sermon",all:1,t:"The Sermon on the Mount",e:"⛰️",d:"A week in Jesus' most famous teaching on how his followers live.",days:[
 {t:"Salt and light",r:["matthew",5,13,16],ex:"Jesus doesn't say \"try to become\" salt and light. He says \"You are.\" Salt preserves and gives flavour; light shows the way. Christians aren't meant to hide in a bubble but to make a difference where they are, so that people \"glorify your Father\", not us.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where could you be salt or light this week: at home, campus, work or online?"}},
 {t:"Love your enemies",r:["matthew",5,38,48],ex:"Perhaps the hardest words Jesus ever said. Loving enemies doesn't mean pretending wrong is right, or staying in danger. It means wanting their good, refusing revenge and praying for them. God does the same: he sends rain \"upon the just and the unjust\".",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray by name for someone who has hurt you or whom you find difficult."}},
 {t:"In secret",r:["matthew",6,1,6],ex:"Giving, praying and fasting are good, but they can quietly turn into performances. Jesus warns against doing good \"to be seen by them\". Your Father \"seeth in secret\". Some of the most important parts of your faith are the ones nobody will ever post about.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which do you find easiest to keep private?",o:["Giving","Praying","Fasting","None: I like to be noticed","All of them"]}},
 {t:"The Lord's Prayer",r:["matthew",6,7,15],ex:"Jesus gives us the prayer Christians everywhere share. It begins with God (his name, his kingdom, his will) before it turns to our needs: daily bread, forgiveness and protection. Notice the only line Jesus comments on afterwards: forgiving others (vv. 14-15).",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Which line does Jesus explain straight after the prayer?",o:["Thy kingdom come","Forgiving others their offences","Give us this day our bread"],a:1,why:"Matthew 6:14-15. Receiving forgiveness and giving it go together."}},
 {t:"Where your treasure is",r:["matthew",6,19,24],ex:"\"For where thy treasure is, there is thy heart also.\" What we spend money, time and attention on shows what we love. \"Mammon\" means wealth treated like a god. Jesus is clear that you can't serve both, so you will choose one.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What competes most with God for your heart?",o:["Money","My phone","Success","Comfort","A relationship","Others' approval"]}},
 {t:"The speck and the plank",r:["matthew",7,1,5],ex:"Jesus is not saying we can never call wrong wrong. He's warning against the critical spirit that sees others' faults (the \"mote\") while ignoring our own (the \"beam\"). Humility starts with the mirror, not the magnifying glass.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Before criticising someone this week, ask: what's the plank in my own eye?"}},
 {t:"Build on the rock",r:["matthew",7,24,29],ex:"Both houses faced the same storm. The difference wasn't the weather but the foundation. Jesus' point is simple: hearing his words is not enough. The wise person hears \"and doth them\". Which part of this sermon will you actually put into practice?",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which teaching from this week will you build your life on? Name one concrete step."}}]},

{id:"e-elijah",all:1,t:"Elijah: burnout and the still small voice",e:"🔥",d:"A prophet's highs and lows, and how God meets us when we're exhausted.",days:[
 {t:"The widow's flour",r:["1-kings",17,8,16],ex:"(Elias is the Douay-Rheims spelling of Elijah.) In a famine, God sends Elijah to a widow with almost nothing left. She trusts and shares her last meal, and the flour and oil don't run out. God often provides through the people we'd least expect, and asks us to trust before we see.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"When has God provided for you in a way you didn't expect?"}},
 {t:"Fire from heaven",r:["1-kings",18,36,39],ex:"This is Elijah's great victory. He stands alone against hundreds of false prophets, prays a simple prayer, and fire falls. \"The Lord, he is God.\" Remember this high point, because tomorrow's reading begins straight after it.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What did the people cry when the fire fell?",o:["Elias is a great prophet","The Lord, he is God","Let us return to Baal"],a:1,why:"1 Kings 18:39. The miracle points away from Elijah to God."}},
 {t:"Under the juniper tree",r:["1-kings",19,3,8],ex:"Right after his greatest victory, Elijah collapses: afraid, exhausted and wanting to die. God's first response isn't a lecture. An angel lets him sleep, gives him food and water, then lets him sleep again. Sometimes the most spiritual thing you can do is rest and eat. And if you ever feel the way Elijah did, tell someone you trust and get help; you don't have to carry it alone.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What drains you most at the moment?",o:["Studies or work","People's expectations","Lack of sleep","Conflict","Feeling alone","Spiritual dryness"]}},
 {t:"Not in the wind",r:["1-kings",19,9,13],ex:"God wasn't in the hurricane, the earthquake or the fire, but in \"a whistling of a gentle air\" (often translated \"a still small voice\" or \"a gentle whisper\"). We often look for God in the dramatic. He often speaks in quiet, so we need silence to hear him.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Spend five quiet minutes with your phone in another room. What did you notice?"}},
 {t:"You are not alone",r:["1-kings",19,15,18],ex:"Elijah thought \"I alone am left.\" God corrects him: there are seven thousand faithful people he didn't know about. Then God gives him a next step and a companion, Elisha (Eliseus). Burnout says \"it all depends on me.\" God says: you are part of something bigger, and you need others.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who is someone you could lean on (or invite alongside you) this week?"}},
 {t:"Elijah's prayer",r:["james",5,13,18],ex:"James uses Elijah as an example of prayer: \"Elias was a man passible like unto us\", that is, an ordinary person with feelings like ours. His prayers were powerful not because he was a superhero but because God is faithful. The same chapter tells us to pray for one another.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What would help your prayer life most right now?",o:["A set time","A quiet place","Praying with others","A plan for reading Scripture","Shorter, honest prayers"]}},
 {t:"Taken up",r:["2-kings",2,9,12],ex:"Elijah's story ends not in defeat but in glory: he's taken up in a whirlwind, and his mission passes on to Elisha. The man who once wanted to die was carried into heaven. God wasn't finished with him under the juniper tree, and he isn't finished with you.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What has Elijah's story taught you about how God treats tired people?"}}]},

{id:"e-joseph",all:1,t:"Joseph: when life isn't fair",e:"🌈",d:"Betrayal, prison and forgiveness: God at work in a story that looked broken.",days:[
 {t:"Favourite son",r:["genesis",37,3,11],ex:"Joseph's family is a mess: favouritism, jealousy, and a teenager who shares his dreams a little too proudly. God's story often begins in very ordinary, complicated families. Nothing here is beyond his ability to redeem.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What part of your family story do you need to trust God with?"}},
 {t:"Sold by his brothers",r:["genesis",37,23,28],ex:"Stripped, thrown in a pit and sold for twenty pieces of silver. Joseph's own brothers betray him. Christians have long seen in Joseph a picture of Jesus, who was also betrayed for silver. Being treated unfairly doesn't mean God has abandoned you.",act:{k:"poll",q:"When you're treated unfairly, what's your first reaction?",o:["Anger","Withdraw and go quiet","Plan to get even","Pray","Talk to someone","Blame myself"]}},
 {t:"The Lord was with Joseph",r:["genesis",39,20,23],ex:"Joseph does the right thing (he refuses his master's wife) and ends up in prison for it. Yet the key line comes twice: \"the Lord was with Joseph.\" God's presence didn't keep him out of prison; it met him in prison. Joseph kept working faithfully where he was.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does the text repeat about Joseph in prison?",o:["He planned his escape","The Lord was with him","He lost his faith"],a:1,why:"Genesis 39:21, 23: \"the Lord was with him, and made all that he did to prosper.\""}},
 {t:"Not me, but God",r:["genesis",41,14,16],ex:"After years in prison, Joseph finally gets his chance before Pharaoh, and the first thing he does is give the credit away: \"Without me, God shall give Pharao a prosperous answer.\" Hardship had made him humble rather than bitter.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is something good in your life you've been taking credit for that really belongs to God?"}},
 {t:"I am Joseph",r:["genesis",45,3,8],ex:"Joseph now has all the power, and the brothers who sold him are in front of him. Instead of revenge, he weeps and says \"Come nearer to me.\" He can see God's hand: \"God sent me before you… for your preservation.\" That doesn't make their sin good, but God was bigger than it.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there someone you need to say \"come nearer\" to, instead of keeping them at a distance?"}},
 {t:"God meant it for good",r:["genesis",50,15,21],ex:"\"You thought evil against me: but God turned it into good.\" This is one of the Bible's great statements of providence: God working through even the worst human choices to bring about good. Romans 8:28 says the same: all things work together for good for those who love God.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Joseph say God did with his brothers' evil?",o:["Ignored it","Turned it into good","Punished them for it"],a:1,why:"Genesis 50:20, \"God turned it into good, that he might… save many people.\""}},
 {t:"Looking back",r:["romans",8,28,28],ex:"Joseph's story took over twenty years to make sense. Yours might too. Trusting God's providence doesn't mean pretending pain is good; it means believing he is still writing, and that the chapter you're in isn't the last one.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you most need to trust that God is still writing your story?",o:["Family","Studies or career","Relationships","Health","Faith","A loss or disappointment"]}}]},

{id:"e-david",all:1,t:"David: a heart after God",e:"👑",d:"Shepherd, giant-slayer, king, sinner and worshipper: a week with David.",days:[
 {t:"The Lord looks at the heart",r:["1-samuel",16,6,13],ex:"(Isai is the Douay-Rheims spelling of Jesse.) Samuel was impressed by height and looks. God wasn't: \"man seeth those things that appear, but the Lord beholdeth the heart.\" The youngest son, out with the sheep and not even invited, was God's choice.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does the Lord look at, according to verse 7?",o:["Strength","The heart","Family background"],a:1,why:"1 Samuel 16:7, \"the Lord beholdeth the heart.\""}},
 {t:"In the name of the Lord",r:["1-samuel",17,45,50],ex:"David faced Goliath not with Saul's armour but with what God had already trained him with: a sling and stones from his shepherd days. His confidence was not in himself: \"I come to thee in the name of the Lord.\" \"For it is his battle.\"",act:{k:"poll",q:"What \"giant\" are you facing at the moment?",o:["Fear","An exam or deadline","A habit","A difficult person","Doubt","Loneliness"]}},
 {t:"Sparing Saul",r:["1-samuel",24,4,11],ex:"David had the perfect chance to kill the king who was hunting him. His men said it was God's will. David refused, and even felt guilty for cutting Saul's robe. A heart after God leaves justice to God, even when revenge looks justified.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where are you tempted to take matters into your own hands instead of trusting God?"}},
 {t:"Kindness for Jonathan's sake",r:["2-samuel",9,1,7],ex:"(Miphiboseth is Mephibosheth.) David the king searches for someone from his enemy Saul's family, not to destroy them but to show \"the mercy of God\". He finds Jonathan's disabled son and seats him at the king's table for good. It's a beautiful picture of grace: we too are invited to the King's table.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who could you show unexpected kindness to this week?"}},
 {t:"You are the man",r:["2-samuel",12,1,13],ex:"David committed adultery and arranged a murder. God sent Nathan with a story, and David condemned the man in it, until Nathan said \"Thou art the man\" (v. 7). David's greatness isn't that he never sinned. It's that when confronted, he didn't make excuses: \"I have sinned against the Lord.\"",act:{k:"poll",q:"When someone points out a fault in you, what's your usual response?",o:["Defend myself","Deflect or blame","Go quiet","Admit it","Depends who says it"]}},
 {t:"Create a clean heart",r:["psalms",50,3,14],ex:"(This is Psalm 51 in most Bibles.) David's prayer after his sin. He doesn't hide or minimise it, and he doesn't despair: he throws himself on God's mercy. Millions have prayed these words. Verse 12 is the heart of it: \"Create a clean heart in me, O God.\"",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray verse 12 slowly. Is there something you need to bring to God's mercy today?"}},
 {t:"The Lord is my shepherd",r:["psalms",22,1,6],ex:"(This is Psalm 23 in most Bibles.) The shepherd boy who became king knew that he too needed a Shepherd. After a week of David's highs and lows, finish with his most loved psalm: God leads, restores, walks with us through the darkest valley, and brings us home.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which line of this psalm will you carry with you this week?"}}]},

{id:"e-james",all:1,t:"Faith that works: James",e:"🛠️",d:"Seven days in the Bible's most practical letter.",days:[
 {t:"Joy in trials",r:["james",1,2,6],ex:"James doesn't say trials are fun. He says they can produce something: \"the trying of your faith worketh patience\". God wastes nothing. And if you don't know what to do, ask; God gives wisdom generously \"and upbraideth not\", that is, without scolding you for asking.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What trial are you in, and what might God be growing in you through it?"}},
 {t:"Doers, not just hearers",r:["james",1,19,27],ex:"Quick to listen, slow to speak, slow to anger: a recipe for better relationships and better online behaviour. James compares hearing God's word without doing it to looking in a mirror and forgetting your own face. Real religion, he says, cares for orphans and widows and stays unstained by the world.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which is hardest for you?",o:["Swift to hear","Slow to speak","Slow to anger"]}},
 {t:"No favourites",r:["james",2,1,5],ex:"Welcoming the rich and sidelining the poor was a problem in the first churches too. James calls it judging with \"unjust thoughts\". In a country as unequal as South Africa, this is very practical: how do we treat the car guard, the cleaner, the new student who doesn't fit in?",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Who gets overlooked in your circles? Do one thing to include them this week."}},
 {t:"Faith without works is dead",r:["james",2,14,26],ex:"James and Paul are not contradicting each other. Paul says we can't earn salvation by works; James says real faith can't help showing itself in works. A \"faith\" that never feeds the hungry is like a body without breath (v. 26). We're saved by grace through a living faith, and living faith acts.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does James say faith without works is?",o:["Weak but enough","Dead","Hidden"],a:1,why:"James 2:17, 26: \"faith without works is dead.\""}},
 {t:"Taming the tongue",r:["james",3,3,10],ex:"A small bit steers a horse; a small rudder steers a ship; a small spark burns a forest. The tongue (and today, the keyboard) has the same power. \"Out of the same mouth proceedeth blessing and cursing.\" James's point: words are never \"just words\".",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do your words most need taming?",o:["Gossip","Sarcasm","Complaining","Anger","Online comments","Criticism of myself"]}},
 {t:"If the Lord wills",r:["james",4,13,17],ex:"Planning is good. Arrogant planning, as if tomorrow were guaranteed, isn't. \"For what is your life? It is a vapour.\" Saying \"if the Lord will\" is not a superstition but an attitude: holding our plans with open hands.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Which plan for the year ahead do you need to hand over to God?"}},
 {t:"Pray for one another",r:["james",5,13,16],ex:"James ends with prayer for every situation: sad, cheerful, sick or burdened by sin. Christians should pray for the sick, and Catholics see the anointing of the sick here. \"Confess… your sins one to another\" reminds us that faith isn't meant to be lived alone. \"The continual prayer of a just man availeth much.\"",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Share one prayer request with the group, and pray for someone else's."}}]},

{id:"e-philippians",all:1,t:"Joy in hard places: Philippians",e:"😊",d:"A letter about joy written from a prison cell.",days:[
 {t:"He will finish it",r:["philippians",1,3,11],ex:"Paul is in chains, yet he begins with thanks and joy. His confidence for his friends is in God, not them: \"he who hath begun a good work in you will perfect it.\" Your growth is God's project. He doesn't start things he can't finish.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What good work has God begun in you that you want him to finish?"}},
 {t:"To live is Christ",r:["philippians",1,20,26],ex:"\"For to me, to live is Christ: and to die is gain.\" Paul doesn't know whether he'll be executed, and he's at peace either way. When Christ is the centre, even death loses its power to terrify. How would you finish the sentence \"For me, to live is…\"?",act:{k:"poll",q:"Honestly, what is life most about for you right now?",o:["Studies or career","Relationships","Faith","Enjoyment","Family","Figuring it out"]}},
 {t:"He humbled himself",r:["philippians",2,5,11],ex:"Possibly an early Christian hymn. The Son of God \"emptied himself, taking the form of a servant\", all the way to the Cross, and was then exalted above every name. Paul's point: \"let this mind be in you.\" Humility isn't thinking less of yourself; it's thinking of others the way Jesus did.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What did Christ take, according to verse 7?",o:["The form of a servant","The throne of David","The power of Rome"],a:0,why:"Philippians 2:7, \"emptied himself, taking the form of a servant.\""}},
 {t:"Counting it loss",r:["philippians",3,7,14],ex:"Paul had an impressive religious CV. He calls it all loss, even \"dung\" (yes, really), compared to knowing Christ. But he hasn't arrived: \"forgetting the things that are behind… I press towards the mark.\" The Christian life is a race we keep running.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What \"things that are behind\" do you need to stop looking back at?"}},
 {t:"Rejoice always",r:["philippians",4,4,9],ex:"\"Rejoice in the Lord always.\" This is not \"always feel happy\". It's joy rooted in the Lord, which can sit alongside sadness. Paul links it to gentleness, prayer instead of worry, and choosing what we think about.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write down three things that are \"true… lovely… of good fame\" in your life today."}},
 {t:"The secret of contentment",r:["philippians",4,10,13],ex:"\"I can do all things in him who strengtheneth me\" is often quoted for sports and exams. In context it's about contentment: Paul has learned to be at peace both hungry and full. Christ's strength helps us cope with whatever we have, not only to win.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you find contentment hardest?",o:["Money","Looks","Relationships","Achievements","Where I live","Comparing myself online"]}},
 {t:"Shine like stars",r:["philippians",2,12,16],ex:"\"With fear and trembling work out your salvation. For it is God who worketh in you.\" Both are true at once: we really work, and God is really at work in our working. Paul pictures Christians shining \"as lights in the world\" in a crooked generation.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What has this letter taught you about joy?"}}]},

{id:"e-iam",all:1,t:"The \"I am\" sayings of Jesus",e:"✨",d:"Seven sayings in John's Gospel where Jesus tells us who he is.",days:[
 {t:"I am the bread of life",r:["john",6,35,40],ex:"Jesus doesn't just give bread; he is bread. Everyone hungers for meaning, love and purpose. \"He that cometh to me shall not hunger.\" Later in the chapter Jesus speaks of giving his flesh for the life of the world, which the Church has always connected with the Lord's Supper.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What do people around you hunger for most?",o:["Love","Meaning","Success","Security","Belonging","Peace"]}},
 {t:"I am the light of the world",r:["john",8,12,12],ex:"Said during a festival when huge lamps lit up the Temple. \"He that followeth me walketh not in darkness.\" Light doesn't remove every question, but it shows you the next step on the path.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need Jesus' light right now?"}},
 {t:"I am the door",r:["john",10,7,10],ex:"A shepherd would sometimes sleep across the entrance of the sheepfold: he himself was the door. Jesus is the way in to safety and life. And he came not to shrink our lives but so \"that they may have life and may have it more abundantly.\"",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Why does Jesus say he came, in verse 10?",o:["To make life harder","That they may have life more abundantly","To start a new religion"],a:1,why:"John 10:10."}},
 {t:"I am the good shepherd",r:["john",10,11,16],ex:"Hired hands run when the wolf comes. The good shepherd \"giveth his life for his sheep\". Jesus knows his own by name, and he speaks of \"other sheep\" who will become \"one fold and one shepherd\", a prayer for unity among all his followers.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray today for unity among Christians: Catholic, evangelical, Orthodox and every church."}},
 {t:"I am the resurrection",r:["john",11,21,27],ex:"Martha believes in a resurrection \"at the last day\". Jesus brings it into the present: \"I am the resurrection and the life.\" Then he asks her, and us: \"Believest thou this?\" Martha's answer is one of the great confessions of faith in the Gospels.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What gives you the most hope when someone dies?",o:["The resurrection","Heaven","Seeing them again","God's mercy","I struggle with this"]}},
 {t:"I am the way, the truth and the life",r:["john",14,5,7],ex:"Thomas wants directions; Jesus gives himself. Not a way among many, but \"the way, and the truth, and the life\". Christianity is not first a set of rules or ideas but a relationship with a Person.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"How would you explain to a friend why you follow Jesus?"}},
 {t:"Before Abraham was, I AM",r:["john",8,56,58],ex:"\"I AM\" is the name God revealed to Moses at the burning bush (Exodus 3:14). Jesus applies it to himself, and his hearers understood: they picked up stones (v. 59). Every \"I am\" saying this week points here: Jesus is God with us.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What name did God reveal to Moses that Jesus uses here?",o:["I AM","The Most High","The Holy One"],a:0,why:"Exodus 3:14; John 8:58."}}]},

{id:"e-parables",all:1,t:"Stories Jesus told",e:"🌱",d:"Seven parables about God's kingdom and what it asks of us.",days:[
 {t:"The sower",r:["matthew",13,3,9],ex:"Same seed, four kinds of soil. The seed (God's word) is fine; the difference is how it's received. Hard paths, shallow rock and choking thorns are all things we can recognise in ourselves. Jesus explains it later in the chapter (vv. 18-23).",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which soil describes your heart this season?",o:["Hard path","Stony ground","Thorns","Good soil","A mix"]}},
 {t:"The mustard seed and the leaven",r:["matthew",13,31,33],ex:"God's kingdom starts small and grows quietly but unstoppably, like a tiny seed becoming a tree or a pinch of yeast working through all the dough. Don't despise small beginnings: a short prayer, one act of kindness, a small group of friends.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What small thing could you do faithfully every day?"}},
 {t:"Treasure and pearl",r:["matthew",13,44,46],ex:"Both men sell everything, and the first does it \"for joy\". Following Jesus costs something, but it's not grim sacrifice. It's what you do when you've found something worth more than all you have.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would you find hardest to give up for Christ?"}},
 {t:"The Good Samaritan",r:["luke",10,29,37],ex:"The question was \"who is my neighbour?\" Jesus turns it around: who acted like a neighbour? The hero is a Samaritan, the outsider his audience looked down on. The religious professionals walked past. Love isn't about who qualifies; it's about who's in front of you.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Who stopped to help the wounded man?",o:["A priest","A Levite","A Samaritan"],a:2,why:"Luke 10:33. Jesus' hearers would have been shocked."}},
 {t:"Two men praying",r:["luke",18,9,14],ex:"The Pharisee's prayer is a list of his own achievements. The tax collector (\"publican\") can only say \"O God, be merciful to me a sinner.\" Jesus says it was the second who went home justified. God resists the proud and welcomes the honest.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray the tax collector's prayer today: \"O God, be merciful to me a sinner.\""}},
 {t:"The rich fool",r:["luke",12,15,21],ex:"The man's problem wasn't that he was rich. It was that he talked only to himself (\"my fruits… my barns… my soul\") and planned as if he would live for ever. \"Thou fool, this night do they require thy soul of thee.\" Be \"rich towards God\".",act:{k:"poll",q:"What does being \"rich towards God\" look like for a student or young worker?",o:["Giving regularly","Generous with time","Praying daily","Serving others","Living simply"]}},
 {t:"The talents",r:["matthew",25,14,21],ex:"A talent was a huge sum of money, but the parable speaks to every gift we've been given. The master entrusts different amounts \"according to his proper ability\". What matters isn't how much you've received but what you do with it. \"Well done, good and faithful servant.\"",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name one gift God has given you. How will you use it for others this week?"}}]},

{id:"e-share",all:1,t:"Sharing your faith",e:"📣",d:"A week on talking about Jesus naturally, kindly and without fear.",days:[
 {t:"Go and make disciples",r:["matthew",28,18,20],ex:"The Great Commission starts with Jesus' authority and ends with his presence: \"I am with you all days.\" We're not sent alone. Making disciples includes baptising and teaching, which is more than one conversation: it's walking with people.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What stops you most from talking about your faith?",o:["Fear of rejection","Not knowing what to say","Not wanting to seem pushy","Feeling like a hypocrite","Nobody asks"]}},
 {t:"Ready with a reason",r:["1-peter",3,15,16],ex:"Be ready to give \"a reason of that hope which is in you\", but \"with modesty and fear\" (gentleness and respect). Our tone matters as much as our arguments. People rarely become Christians because they lost a debate.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"In two or three sentences, write why you have hope in Jesus."}},
 {t:"Come and see",r:["john",4,28,30],ex:"The Samaritan woman had a past and no theology degree. She just told people what Jesus had done for her and invited them: \"Come, and see.\" Your story of what God has done is something nobody can argue with.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What's one thing Jesus has done in your life that you could share?"}},
 {t:"Now we believe for ourselves",r:["john",4,39,42],ex:"Her testimony brought people to Jesus, but then they met him for themselves: \"we ourselves have heard him.\" Our job is to introduce people to Jesus, not to replace him. Faith becomes theirs when they meet him personally.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"Why did many Samaritans first believe?",o:["Because of a miracle","Because of the woman's testimony","Because of the disciples' preaching"],a:1,why:"John 4:39, \"for the word of the woman giving testimony.\""}},
 {t:"Paul in Athens",r:["acts",17,22,28],ex:"Paul starts where his listeners are, with their own altar \"To the Unknown God\" and even their own poets. He's respectful but clear. It's a model for talking about faith with people who see things very differently: listen, find common ground, then point to Christ.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What \"unknown god\" do people you know search for (meaning, success, love)? How might Jesus answer that longing?"}},
 {t:"How will they hear?",r:["romans",10,13,15],ex:"\"Whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved.\" But people can't call on someone they've never heard of. Paul calls the feet of those who bring good news \"beautiful\". Sometimes you are the only Bible someone will read.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which way of sharing faith suits you best?",o:["Conversations","Inviting people to church or group","Social media","Acts of service","Praying for people","Answering questions"]}},
 {t:"Seasoned with salt",r:["colossians",4,5,6],ex:"\"Let your speech be always in grace seasoned with salt.\" Gracious, interesting and wise, not preachy or bland. Pray for opportunities, and then just be a kind, honest friend who is unashamed of Jesus.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Name one person you'll pray for by name every day this month."}}]},

{id:"e-psalms",all:1,t:"Praying the Psalms",e:"🎵",d:"Seven psalms for seven moods: joy, wonder, longing, fear, thanks and praise.",days:[
 {t:"Two ways (Psalm 1)",r:["psalms",1,1,6],ex:"The Psalms open with a choice between two paths. The happy person is like a tree planted by water, rooted in God's word, fruitful in season and green in drought. The other way blows away like chaff. Which roots are you putting down?",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What's one habit that would plant you \"near the running waters\" of God's word?"}},
 {t:"What is man? (Psalm 8)",r:["psalms",8,2,10],ex:"Looking at the night sky, David feels small, and then amazed that God cares for us anyway, crowning us \"with glory and honour\". The Psalms let us hold both: we're tiny, and we're loved.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Go outside tonight and look at the sky for two minutes. Then pray verse 5."}},
 {t:"The heavens declare (Psalm 19)",r:["psalms",18,2,7],ex:"(The Douay-Rheims counts this as Psalm 18; most Bibles call it Psalm 19.) Creation preaches without words: \"The heavens shew forth the glory of God.\" Science and faith aren't enemies; the more we understand the universe, the more there is to wonder at.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you feel closest to God in creation?",o:["Mountains","The sea","The night sky","The bush","A garden","A thunderstorm"]}},
 {t:"As the deer longs (Psalm 42)",r:["psalms",41,2,6],ex:"(Psalm 41 in the Douay-Rheims.) A psalm for when God feels far away. \"My tears have been my bread day and night.\" The psalmist talks to his own soul: \"Why art thou sad, O my soul?… Hope in God.\" The Psalms give us permission to be honest about sadness, and hope in the middle of it.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Write your own short lament: what's heavy, and where you'll put your hope."}},
 {t:"Under his wings (Psalm 91)",r:["psalms",90,1,11],ex:"(Psalm 90 in the Douay-Rheims.) A psalm of protection, prayed by many Christians at night. God is a refuge and fortress; \"he will overshadow thee with his shoulders\" like a bird sheltering her young. Jesus was tempted with verse 11 in the desert and refused to test God with it: trust isn't recklessness.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pray this psalm before you sleep tonight, for yourself and someone you love."}},
 {t:"Bless the Lord, O my soul (Psalm 103)",r:["psalms",102,1,13],ex:"(Psalm 102 in the Douay-Rheims.) A thanksgiving psalm: \"never forget all he hath done for thee.\" God forgives, heals, redeems and crowns us with mercy. \"As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our iniquities from us.\"",act:{k:"reflect",q:"List five things God has done for you. Read them back as a prayer of thanks."}},
 {t:"Let everything praise (Psalm 150)",r:["psalms",150,1,5],ex:"The Psalms end with nothing but praise: trumpets, harps, dancing, loud cymbals. Whatever style of worship you prefer (hymns, chant, choirs or a worship band), the last word goes to this: \"Let every spirit praise the Lord. Alleluia.\"",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you love to worship most?",o:["Hymns","Worship band","Silence","Chant","Choir","Singing alone"]}}]},

{id:"e-forgive",all:1,t:"Forgiveness",e:"🤝",d:"A week on receiving God's forgiveness and learning to forgive others.",days:[
 {t:"Seventy times seven",r:["matthew",18,21,35],ex:"Peter thought seven times was generous. Jesus says seventy times seven: stop counting. The parable shows why: we've been forgiven a debt we could never repay, so how can we refuse to forgive the much smaller debts others owe us?",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How many times does Jesus tell Peter to forgive?",o:["Seven times","Seventy times seven times","Until they apologise"],a:1,why:"Matthew 18:22. In other words, without keeping score."}},
 {t:"Father, forgive them",r:["luke",23,33,34],ex:"While being crucified, Jesus prays for the people doing it. He doesn't pretend what they're doing is fine. He hands it to the Father and asks for their forgiveness. Forgiveness isn't saying \"it didn't matter\"; it's refusing to let hatred have the last word.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there someone you can't yet forgive? Can you at least pray, \"Father, forgive them\"?"}},
 {t:"As the Lord forgave you",r:["colossians",3,12,15],ex:"\"Even as the Lord hath forgiven you, so do you also.\" The measure of our forgiveness is God's forgiveness of us. Paul lists the clothes Christians put on (mercy, kindness, humility, patience) and ties them together with love and peace.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What makes forgiving hardest for you?",o:["They're not sorry","It keeps happening","It hurt someone I love","I want justice","I'm still angry","I don't know how"]}},
 {t:"Go first and be reconciled",r:["matthew",5,23,24],ex:"Jesus says reconciliation is more urgent than worship: leave your gift at the altar and go make peace first. Notice who moves first: the person who remembers that \"thy brother hath anything against thee\". Sometimes we need to ask for forgiveness, not only give it.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Is there someone you need to apologise to? What's one step you can take?"}},
 {t:"She loved much",r:["luke",7,44,50],ex:"A woman known as a sinner washes Jesus' feet with her tears. The religious host is offended. Jesus says the one forgiven much loves much. The more we grasp how much God has forgiven us, the more love (and the more mercy) flows out of us.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Thank God today for specific things he has forgiven you."}},
 {t:"What forgiveness is not",r:["romans",12,17,21],ex:"\"Revenge not yourselves… Revenge is mine, I will repay, saith the Lord.\" Forgiving is not excusing, forgetting, or staying in an unsafe situation. It's handing the right to revenge over to God, who is just. If someone has abused you, forgiveness never means you must keep quiet or go back; seeking safety and help is right. Psychology research also suggests that forgiveness, worked through over time, tends to bring less anger and better well-being.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which statement surprised you?",o:["Forgiving isn't excusing","Forgiving isn't forgetting","Forgiving isn't always reconciling","Forgiveness can take time","God takes justice seriously"]}},
 {t:"Overcome evil with good",r:["romans",12,14,21],ex:"\"Be not overcome by evil, but overcome evil by good.\" Forgiveness is a process, often repeated many times over the same hurt. Ask God for the grace to take the next small step, and remember you're also receiving the mercy you're learning to give.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What one step of forgiveness (or reconciliation) will you take this week?"}}]},

{id:"e-daniel",all:1,t:"Daniel: faithful in Babylon",e:"🦁",d:"How to stay faithful when the culture around you doesn't share your faith.",days:[
 {t:"Daniel purposed in his heart",r:["daniel",1,8,16],ex:"Daniel and his friends were taken to a foreign land, given new names and pressured to fit in. Daniel decided in advance where he would draw the line, and asked respectfully rather than rebelling. Decide your convictions before the pressure comes.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What is one line you've decided in advance you won't cross?"}},
 {t:"Even if he does not",r:["daniel",3,14,18],ex:"(Sidrach, Misach and Abdenago are Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego.) Their answer to the king is one of the bravest in Scripture: our God is able to save us, but even if he doesn't, we still won't bow. Faith isn't a deal with God; it's loyalty to him whatever happens.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Where do you feel most pressure to \"bow\" and fit in?",o:["Campus or school","Work","Friends","Social media","Family","Dating"]}},
 {t:"Four men in the fire",r:["daniel",3,91,95],ex:"(The Douay-Rheims includes the young men's song in the fire, so this chapter's verse numbers are higher than in most Bibles.) Three were thrown in, but the king sees four walking free, the fourth \"like the son of God\". God didn't keep them out of the fire; he met them in it.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"How many men did the king see in the furnace?",o:["Three","Four","Seven"],a:1,why:"Daniel 3:92. God was with them in the fire."}},
 {t:"Three times a day",r:["daniel",6,10,10],ex:"When praying was made illegal, Daniel didn't make a show and didn't hide. He simply prayed as he \"had been accustomed to do before\". His courage under pressure came from habits built over years. What you do every day is what you'll do when it's hard.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What daily prayer habit do you want to build? Pick a time and place."}},
 {t:"In the lions' den",r:["daniel",6,16,23],ex:"Even the king hoped Daniel's God would save him, and he did. Daniel's explanation is simple: God sent his angel and shut the lions' mouths. Faithfulness may cost us, but God sees and is able to deliver.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Where do you need courage to be faithful this week?"}},
 {t:"Seek the welfare of the city",r:["jeremiah",29,4,7],ex:"Jeremiah wrote to the exiles in Babylon: build houses, plant gardens, and \"seek the peace of the city\" and pray for it. Christians in a secular culture aren't meant to withdraw or despise it, but to bless it while staying faithful.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How could Christians best serve our city or campus?",o:["Feeding the hungry","Mentoring and tutoring","Praying for leaders","Excellent work","Caring for the lonely","Clean-ups"]}},
 {t:"Shine like stars",r:["daniel",12,2,3],ex:"Daniel ends with the hope of resurrection: some will wake \"unto life everlasting\", and \"they that instruct many to justice\" will shine \"as stars for all eternity\". Faithfulness in exile isn't wasted. It shines for ever.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What has Daniel taught you about living faithfully in today's world?"}}]},

{id:"e-money",all:1,t:"Money, work and generosity",e:"💰",d:"What the Bible says about money, contentment, work and giving.",days:[
 {t:"Godliness with contentment",r:["1-timothy",6,6,10],ex:"\"We brought nothing into this world: and certainly we can carry nothing out.\" Money isn't evil; \"the desire of money is the root of all evils\" (v. 10). Contentment is a skill we learn, not a feeling we wait for.",act:{k:"quiz",q:"What does Paul say is the root of all evils?",o:["Money","The desire of money","Poverty"],a:1,why:"1 Timothy 6:10. It's the love of money, not money itself."}},
 {t:"Rich in good works",r:["1-timothy",6,17,19],ex:"To those who have more than they need: don't be proud, don't trust money, but \"do good… give easily\". By global standards many of us are wealthier than we think. Wealth is a tool to bless others.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What do you have more than enough of (money, time, skills, things) that you could share?"}},
 {t:"A cheerful giver",r:["2-corinthians",9,6,8],ex:"\"God loveth a cheerful giver.\" Giving isn't about guilt or a fixed rule but a decision made \"in his heart\". Generosity is like sowing: you don't lose seed; you plant it. Many Christians aim for a regular percentage as a habit of trust.",act:{k:"poll",q:"How do you approach giving?",o:["A set percentage","When I see a need","I'd like to start","Mostly time, not money","Not sure how much is right"]}},
 {t:"The widow's two coins",r:["mark",12,41,44],ex:"The rich gave large amounts from their surplus; the widow gave two tiny coins, everything she had. Jesus says she gave more. God measures generosity by sacrifice and love, not size.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What would a gift that really costs you something look like this week?"}},
 {t:"Faithful in little",r:["luke",16,10,13],ex:"\"He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in that which is greater.\" How we handle student budgets, small jobs and other people's property trains us for bigger responsibility. And again: \"You cannot serve God and mammon.\"",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which money habit would you most like to grow in?",o:["Budgeting","Saving","Giving","Avoiding debt","Honesty","Spending less on impulse"]}},
 {t:"Work as for the Lord",r:["colossians",3,23,24],ex:"\"Whatsoever you do, do it from the heart, as to the Lord.\" Your assignments, shifts and side hustles can be worship. Excellence and honesty in ordinary work are a way of serving Christ, whether anyone notices or not.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Pick one task this week and do it as if Jesus were your boss."}},
 {t:"Where your treasure is",r:["luke",12,32,34],ex:"\"Fear not, little flock, for it hath pleased your Father to give you a kingdom.\" Jesus' teaching on money ends with the Father's generosity: because God gives so freely, we can hold money loosely and give freely too.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"What one change in how you use money will you make after this week?"}}]},

{id:"e-rest",all:1,t:"Rest for the weary",e:"😴",d:"Seven days on Sabbath, sleep and the rest Jesus offers.",days:[
 {t:"Come to me",r:["matthew",11,28,30],ex:"\"Come to me all you that labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you.\" Jesus' invitation is to a person, not a technique. His \"yoke\" (the way of life he gives) is \"sweet\" and his burden light, because he carries it with us.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What's making you most weary at the moment?",o:["Studies or work","Responsibilities at home","Too little sleep","Emotional stress","Busyness","Spiritual dryness"]}},
 {t:"God rested",r:["genesis",2,1,3],ex:"God didn't rest because he was tired. He rested to bless and enjoy what he had made, and to set a pattern for us. Rest isn't laziness; it's part of how we were designed.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"When did you last stop and simply enjoy something good God made?"}},
 {t:"Remember the Sabbath",r:["exodus",20,8,11],ex:"The Sabbath command protected everyone, including servants, foreigners and even animals, from endless work. Christians keep the Lord's Day on Sunday, the day of the resurrection. Worship and real rest one day a week is a gift, not a burden.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Plan this Sunday: when will you worship, and what will you do (or not do) to really rest?"}},
 {t:"Come apart and rest",r:["mark",6,30,32],ex:"The apostles came back excited from ministry, and Jesus' response was: \"Come apart into a desert place, and rest a little.\" They hadn't even had time to eat. Even good work needs breaks. Jesus himself often withdrew to quiet places to pray.",act:{k:"poll",q:"What actually recharges you?",o:["Time alone","Time with friends","Nature","Sleep","Sport","Prayer and quiet","Music"]}},
 {t:"He gives his beloved sleep",r:["psalms",126,1,2],ex:"(Psalm 127 in most Bibles.) \"Unless the Lord build the house, they labour in vain that build it.\" Staying up late and getting up early doesn't make us more secure; God does. Sleep is an act of trust. Good sleep also protects mood, memory and concentration, which matters at exam time.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Try a phone-free last 30 minutes before bed this week. Notice any difference."}},
 {t:"In peace I will sleep",r:["psalms",4,7,9],ex:"\"In peace in the self same I will sleep, and I will rest.\" This short psalm has long been prayed at night. It's a way of handing the day back to God and trusting him with whatever you didn't finish.",act:{k:"reflect",q:"Before sleeping tonight, name three good things from the day and hand the rest to God."}},
 {t:"A rest that remains",r:["hebrews",4,9,11],ex:"\"There remaineth therefore a day of rest for the people of God.\" Every weekend, holiday and good night's sleep is a small taste of the final rest of heaven. One day the weariness will end for good.",act:{k:"poll",q:"Which rest habit will you keep after this week?",o:["Sunday as a real day of rest","Better sleep routine","Daily quiet time","Phone-free times","Time outdoors","Saying no more often"]}}]}
);

;PACK_DONE("studies");
